// ⭐ **IT IS THE TARGET'S BOUND**, mirroring `stdlib/Process.maxon`: `u32.max` on Windows, `0 to
// 255` everywhere else — a POSIX exit status is a byte, and WASI's `wasi:cli/exit.exit-with-code`
// takes a `u8`.
//
// ⭐ **THIS IS A MIRROR OF A DECLARATION, NOT A SECOND AUTHORITY.** `lookup`'s ORDER makes it so:
// the builtin is its LAST arm, BELOW every declaration, so any file declaring `ExitCode` —
// `stdlib/Process.maxon` among them — answers instead, and this is consulted only for a name no
// file declared.
//
// ⭐ **THE BOUND IS RESOLVED WHERE THE TARGET IS KNOWN — AT REGISTRY CONSTRUCTION.** `lookup` holds
// no project and no target; `Project` holds one, and its own header states the licence: a project
// is aimed once and never re-aimed — two targets are two `Project`s. A single shared, target-free
// answer would bake the BUILD MACHINE's OS into a cross-compiled binary's stdlib, and `#if os(...)`
// must resolve against the target OS rather than the host for the same reason.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE RANGE NARROWS; THE SLOT DOES NOT.** The runtime half of the call-argument door sits at
// the CALLEE'S ENTRY — one guard covering every call site, direct, indirect and witness alike
// (`IR/Std/InsertRangeChecks.maxon:379-397`) — and an entry guard reads the value out of the SLOT,
// after the ABI has already narrowed it. ⇒ **the slot must be WIDER than the range or the guard
// cannot see the truncation that already happened.** With a `u8` slot on wasm,
// `takes(opaque(0) - 5)` arrives as 251, which is INSIDE `0 to 255`; the guard would pass and the
// wrong answer would be silent. A width that followed the range (`u32` on Windows, `u8` elsewhere)
// is therefore not available here.
//
// ⇒ `valueTagToStdType` keeps `ExitCode` at `StdType.u32` and `primitiveTypeByteSize(exitCode)` keeps 4,
// on EVERY target, and neither may start to derive from the bounds below. A guard narrower than its slot
// is what every ranged alias in the language already is — `int(0 to 100)` rides an i64, and `lookup`'s
// header spells that argument out in full.
let ExitCodeAliasLow = 0 as ParsedInt

// The byte-sized POSIX status shared by Linux, macOS and WASI — the `#else` arm of the declaration
// mirrored above.
let PosixExitCodeAliasHigh = 255 as ParsedInt

// The platform shape, spelled ONCE. A `match` over `Os` rather than a "Windows or not" test, for
// `cpuCanonicalName`'s reason: a fifth OS must be unable to arrive without an arm being written here,
// where an `else` would quietly hand it Windows' bound or POSIX's.
function exitCodeAliasHigh(os Os) returns ParsedInt
	return match os 'os'
		windows gives U32MaxBound
		linux or
			macos or
			wasi gives PosixExitCodeAliasHigh
	end 'os'
end 'exitCodeAliasHigh'

// ⭐ **THE RANGE MUST FIT THE SLOT, AND THIS IS THE CHECK BETWEEN THE TWO COPIES.**
// `primitiveTypeByteSize(exitCode)` is the slot's width written in the layout's vocabulary, and nothing
// derives it from the bound above — so what keeps the two together is that a change to either is caught
// HERE rather than surfacing as a guard admitting values the storage cannot hold. Asked once per registry
// construction, every one of which is on a cold path.
//
// ⚠ **IT TESTS FIT, NOT EQUALITY.** On a POSIX target `0 to 255` needs ONE byte against a four-byte
// slot, and that inequality is the mechanism rather than a tolerance it has to forgive — the entry
// guard above can only separate a truncated value from an honest one while range < slot. (At u32
// width on Windows nothing truncates on the way in, so there is nothing to separate.) What is an
// error is a range the slot CANNOT hold — a guard admitting values that would be silently cut down
// on every store.
function requireExitCodeRangeFitsItsWidth(high ParsedInt)
	let rangeBytes = rangedAliasStorageBytes(ExitCodeAliasLow, high: high)
	let widthBytes = primitiveTypeByteSize(ValueTypeTag.exitCode)

	if rangeBytes > widthBytes 'rangeOutgrowsItsWidth'
		panic("requireExitCodeRangeFitsItsWidth: the builtin `ExitCode` range 0 to {high} needs a {rangeBytes}-byte slot but `primitiveTypeByteSize` gives the tag {widthBytes} — a range wider than its own storage guards nothing, because every value it admits is truncated on the way in")
	end 'rangeOutgrowsItsWidth'
end 'requireExitCodeRangeFitsItsWidth'

// ⭐ **THE BUILTIN ALIAS ITSELF — built from the caller's own name and the caller's own file.**
//
// The NAME is the one `lookup` was asked about rather than a fresh `b"ExitCode"`, because
// `builtinTypeNameTag` has already answered that the two are the same bytes; a spelling here would
// be one more copy of a builtin type name outside its table.
//
// ⚠ **THE DECLARING FILE IS THE READER'S OWN, and that is the honest answer rather than a filler.**
// A builtin is declared in NO file and nameable from EVERY one, so the reader's path is the path at
// which it is in scope — visible at every tier, not merely at the `public` one it also carries —
// and no reader of `alias.declFilePath` is ever handed a path the sweep did not visit.
//
// ⚠ **THE HIGH BOUND IS PASSED IN, not read from a constant, because it is the TARGET's** — see the seed
// above. It is the registry's, resolved once when the registry was built, so every lookup in one compile
// answers with one platform's range by construction rather than by every caller remembering to ask.
//
// ⚠ `public`, not `global`: a `public` library signature names `ExitCode`, and at `global` E3167 would refuse it.
function builtinExitCodeAlias(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath, high ParsedInt) returns RangedTypeAlias
	return RangedTypeAlias.create(name, underlying: MaxonType.integer, low: ExitCodeAliasLow, high: high, visibility: Visibility.public, declFilePath: readerFilePath, nameLine: 0, nameColumn: 0)
end 'builtinExitCodeAlias'

typealias ScopedAliasTier = int(0 to 3)

let ScopedAliasTierHiddenFromTheReader = 0 as ScopedAliasTier
let ScopedAliasTierTheLibrarys = 1 as ScopedAliasTier
let ScopedAliasTierSomeAuthors = 2 as ScopedAliasTier
let ScopedAliasTierReadersOwnFile = 3 as ScopedAliasTier

// How near a claimant of a contested type name is to the file reading it — the tier half of
// `ClaimantProvenance.rankedClaimIn`, the ONE ranking behind every scoped type-name door.
//
// ⚠ **ANOTHER AUTHOR'S FILE OUTRANKS THE LIBRARY FOR AN AUTHOR READER, AND IS HIDDEN FROM A LIBRARY
// READER.** An author's declaration of a library name is what that name means to every author file,
// while the library's own files keep meaning the library's. A file that declares no `Byte` of its own
// means a sibling user file's, which `specs/bytearray-element-size.md`'s
// `error.a-byte-string-literal-takes-the-sibling-files-wide-byte-it-names-bare` pins: the element width a
// literal is folded at follows this answer.
//
// ⛔ **A TIE IN TIER GOES TO THE DECLARATION THAT FOLDS LATER** (`foldsLaterThan`): the later layer —
// authors, then library, the sweep's order — and within one layer the byte-wise later project-relative
// path (`ClaimantProvenance.foldKeyOf`). A tie broken any other way would read a hash map's bucket order,
// which is no fact about the program.
function scopedAliasTier(declPath String, readerPath String, declaredByTheCorpus bool, readerIsTheCorpus bool, visibleToReader bool) returns ScopedAliasTier
	if declPath.equals(readerPath) 'theReadersOwnDeclaration'
		return ScopedAliasTierReadersOwnFile
	end 'theReadersOwnDeclaration'

	if not visibleToReader or (readerIsTheCorpus and not declaredByTheCorpus) 'hiddenFromThisReader'
		return ScopedAliasTierHiddenFromTheReader
	end 'hiddenFromThisReader'

	return ScopedAliasTierTheLibrarys if declaredByTheCorpus else ScopedAliasTierSomeAuthors
end 'scopedAliasTier'

export typealias FoldLayer = int(0 to 1)

let FoldLayerAuthors = 0 as FoldLayer
let FoldLayerLibrary = 1 as FoldLayer

function foldLayerFor(declaredByTheCorpus bool) returns FoldLayer
	return FoldLayerLibrary if declaredByTheCorpus else FoldLayerAuthors
end 'foldLayerFor'

type ClaimantRanking
	var bestTier as ScopedAliasTier
	var bestPosition as FoldPosition

	static function seededWith(tier ScopedAliasTier, position FoldPosition) returns ClaimantRanking
		return Self{bestTier: tier, bestPosition: position}
	end 'seededWith'

	function admits(tier ScopedAliasTier, position FoldPosition) returns bool
		if not self.isOutrankedBy(tier, position: position) 'theBestSoFarStands'
			return false
		end 'theBestSoFarStands'

		self.bestTier = tier
		self.bestPosition = position
		return true
	end 'admits'

	function winnerIsHiddenFromTheReader() returns bool
		return self.bestTier == ScopedAliasTierHiddenFromTheReader
	end 'winnerIsHiddenFromTheReader'

	function isOutrankedBy(tier ScopedAliasTier, position FoldPosition) returns bool
		if tier != self.bestTier 'differentTiers'
			return tier > self.bestTier
		end 'differentTiers'

		return foldsLaterThan(position, than: self.bestPosition)
	end 'isOutrankedBy'
end 'ClaimantRanking'

export type RankedClaimPosition
	export let at as ElementIndex
	export let hiddenFromTheReader as bool

	export static function create(at ElementIndex, hiddenFromTheReader bool) returns RankedClaimPosition
		return Self{at: at, hiddenFromTheReader: hiddenFromTheReader}
	end 'create'
end 'RankedClaimPosition'

export type ClaimantProvenance
	var moduleDirectories as ModuleDirectoryByFile
	var libraryFiles as LibraryFilePathSet

	export static function create() returns ClaimantProvenance
		return Self{moduleDirectories: ModuleDirectoryByFile.create(), libraryFiles: LibraryFilePathSet.create()}
	end 'create'

	export function noteFingerprint(fingerprint IndexFingerprint)
		fingerprint.note("libraryFiles", value: self.libraryFiles.count())
		fingerprint.note("moduleDirectories", value: self.moduleDirectories.count())
	end 'noteFingerprint'

	export function declaresInTheCorpus(declPath String) returns bool
		return declaredInTheCorpus(self.libraryFiles, declPath: declPath)
	end 'declaresInTheCorpus'

	export function shareFilePartition(directories ModuleDirectoryByFile, libraryPaths LibraryFilePathSet)
		self.moduleDirectories = directories
		self.libraryFiles = libraryPaths
	end 'shareFilePartition'

	export function foldLayerOf(declPath String) returns FoldLayer
		return foldLayerFor(self.declaresInTheCorpus(declPath))
	end 'foldLayerOf'

	module function foldPositionOf(declPath String) returns FoldPosition
		return FoldPosition.create(declPath, key: self.foldKeyOf(declPath), layer: self.foldLayerOf(declPath))
	end 'foldPositionOf'

	function foldKeyOf(declPath String) returns String
		if declPath.equals(CompilerOwnedDeclFilePath.toString()) 'theCompilersOwnDeclaration'
			return declPath
		end 'theCompilersOwnDeclaration'

		return projectRelativeDisplay(moduleDirectoryIn(self.moduleDirectories, path: declPath), path: try FilePath.from(declPath) otherwise panic("ClaimantProvenance.foldKeyOf: '{declPath}' is a declaring-file path and is not a path"))
	end 'foldKeyOf'

	export function foldsLater(declPath String, thanPath String) returns bool
		return foldsLaterThan(self.foldPositionOf(declPath), than: self.foldPositionOf(thanPath))
	end 'foldsLater'

	export function anAuthorDeclaresAny(declPaths StringArray) returns bool
		for declPath in declPaths 'eachDeclaringFile'
			if not self.declaresInTheCorpus(declPath) 'anAuthorsFile'
				return true
			end 'anAuthorsFile'
		end 'eachDeclaringFile'

		return false
	end 'anAuthorDeclaresAny'

	export function ordinalMints(spellings MintByDeclaringFile) returns MintByDeclaringFile
		var sharersOfSpelling = SharersBySpelling.create()

		for (declPath, spelling) in spellings 'eachDeclaration'
			if not sharersOfSpelling.contains(spelling) 'firstDeclarationOfThisSpelling'
				sharersOfSpelling.upsert(spelling, value: StringArray.create())
			end 'firstDeclarationOfThisSpelling'

			var sharers = try sharersOfSpelling.get(spelling) otherwise panic("ClaimantProvenance.ordinalMints: the spelling of '{declPath}' was just seeded into the sharer map and is not there")
			sharers.push(declPath)
		end 'eachDeclaration'

		var minted = MintByDeclaringFile.create()

		for (declPath, spelling) in spellings 'eachMint'
			minted.upsert(declPath, value: self.ordinalMintName(spelling, sharers: try sharersOfSpelling.get(spelling) otherwise panic("ClaimantProvenance.ordinalMints: the spelling of '{declPath}' was grouped in the pass above"), declPath: declPath))
		end 'eachMint'

		return minted
	end 'ordinalMints'

	function ordinalMintName(spelling ByteArray, sharers StringArray, declPath String) returns ByteArray
		if sharers.count() < 2 'theSpellingAloneNamesIt'
			return spelling
		end 'theSpellingAloneNamesIt'

		var place = 1 as ElementIndex

		for other in sharers 'eachSharer'
			if self.foldsLater(declPath, thanPath: other) 'foldsAfterAnotherSharer'
				place = place + 1
			end 'foldsAfterAnotherSharer'
		end 'eachSharer'

		return ordinalMintSpelling(spelling, place: place)
	end 'ordinalMintName'

	export function claimOf(kind TypeDeclKind, declFilePath FilePath, visibility Visibility) returns TypeNameClaim
		return TypeNameClaim.create(kind, declFilePath: declFilePath, visibility: visibility, declaredByTheCorpus: self.declaresInTheCorpus(declFilePath.toString()))
	end 'claimOf'

	export function claimIsVisibleFrom(claim TypeNameClaim, readerPath String) returns bool
		if not isTypeAliasDecl(claim.kind) and (claim.declFilePath.equals(CompilerOwnedDeclFilePath) or readerPath.equals(CompilerOwnedDeclFilePath.toString())) 'theCompilerOwnsOneSide'
			return true
		end 'theCompilerOwnsOneSide'

		return visibleThroughModuleDirectories(self.moduleDirectories, visibility: claim.visibility, declPath: claim.declFilePath.toString(), readerPath: readerPath)
	end 'claimIsVisibleFrom'

	export function rankedDeclaringPath(claims TypeNameClaimArray, readerPath String) returns String
		let ranked = self.rankedClaimIn(claims, readerPath: readerPath)
		return (try claims.get(ranked.at) otherwise panic("ClaimantProvenance.rankedDeclaringPath: the ranked position {ranked.at} is one of the {claims.count()} claims it was ranked among")).declFilePath.toString()
	end 'rankedDeclaringPath'

	export function rankedClaimIn(claims TypeNameClaimArray, readerPath String) returns RankedClaimPosition
		let seedAt = claims.count() - 1
		let seed = try claims.get(seedAt) otherwise panic("ClaimantProvenance.rankedClaimIn: a name's claims are ranked only once at least one declaration holds it")
		var ranking = ClaimantRanking.seededWith(self.tierOfClaim(seed, readerPath: readerPath), position: self.foldPositionOf(seed.declFilePath.toString()))
		var bestAt = seedAt

		for at in 0 upto claims.count() 'eachClaim'
			let claim = try claims.get(at) otherwise panic("ClaimantProvenance.rankedClaimIn: claims.get OOB at {at} inside its own count()")

			if ranking.admits(self.tierOfClaim(claim, readerPath: readerPath), position: self.foldPositionOf(claim.declFilePath.toString())) 'nearerThanTheBestSoFar'
				bestAt = at
			end 'nearerThanTheBestSoFar'
		end 'eachClaim'

		return RankedClaimPosition.create(bestAt, hiddenFromTheReader: ranking.winnerIsHiddenFromTheReader())
	end 'rankedClaimIn'

	function tierOfClaim(claim TypeNameClaim, readerPath String) returns ScopedAliasTier
		return scopedAliasTier(claim.declFilePath.toString(), readerPath: readerPath, declaredByTheCorpus: claim.declaredByTheCorpus, readerIsTheCorpus: self.declaresInTheCorpus(readerPath), visibleToReader: self.claimIsVisibleFrom(claim, readerPath: readerPath))
	end 'tierOfClaim'

	// @generated library-cache begin
	function cacheWrite(writer LibraryCacheWriter)
		cacheWriteModuleDirectoryByFile(writer, value: self.moduleDirectories)
		cacheWriteLibraryFilePathSet(writer, value: self.libraryFiles)
	end 'cacheWrite'

	static function cacheRead(reader LibraryCacheReader) returns ClaimantProvenance throws LibraryCacheDamage
		let f_moduleDirectories = try cacheReadModuleDirectoryByFile(reader)
		let f_libraryFiles = try cacheReadLibraryFilePathSet(reader)

		return Self{moduleDirectories: f_moduleDirectories, libraryFiles: f_libraryFiles}
	end 'cacheRead'

	static function cacheLayoutBytes() returns CacheSize16
		return sizeof(ClaimantProvenance) as CacheSize16
	end 'cacheLayoutBytes'

	// @generated library-cache end
end 'ClaimantProvenance'

type FoldRankedAlias
	module let position as FoldPosition
	module let alias as RangedTypeAlias

	static function create(position FoldPosition, alias RangedTypeAlias) returns FoldRankedAlias
		return Self{position: position, alias: alias}
	end 'create'
end 'FoldRankedAlias'

typealias FoldRankedAliasArray = Array with FoldRankedAlias

function foldRankOrdering(left FoldRankedAlias, right FoldRankedAlias) returns Ordering
	if foldsLaterThan(left.position, than: right.position) 'leftFoldsAfter'
		return Ordering.greaterThan
	end 'leftFoldsAfter'

	return Ordering.lessThan if foldsLaterThan(right.position, than: left.position) else Ordering.equalTo
end 'foldRankOrdering'

module typealias DeclaringFilePathSet = Set with String

function admitEnvironment(environments FilePathArray, seen DeclaringFilePathSet, path FilePath)
	if seen.contains(path.toString()) 'alreadyAnEnvironment'
		return
	end 'alreadyAnEnvironment'

	seen.insert(path.toString())
	environments.push(path)
end 'admitEnvironment'

module type FoldPosition
	module let path as String
	module let key as String
	module let layer as FoldLayer

	static function create(path String, key String, layer FoldLayer) returns FoldPosition
		return Self{path: path, key: key, layer: layer}
	end 'create'
end 'FoldPosition'

function foldsLaterThan(position FoldPosition, than FoldPosition) returns bool
	if position.layer != than.layer 'differentLayers'
		return position.layer > than.layer
	end 'differentLayers'

	if position.path.equals(than.path) 'oneDeclaringFile'
		return false
	end 'oneDeclaringFile'

	if position.key.equals(than.key) 'oneKeyTwoFiles'
		return stringBytesPrecede(than.path, later: position.path)
	end 'oneKeyTwoFiles'

	return stringBytesPrecede(than.key, later: position.key)
end 'foldsLaterThan'

module function declaredInTheCorpus(libraryFiles LibraryFilePathSet, declPath String) returns bool
	return declPath.equals(CompilerOwnedDeclFilePath.toString()) or libraryFiles.contains(declPath)
end 'declaredInTheCorpus'

export function projectRelativeDisplay(moduleDirectory String, path FilePath) returns String
	if moduleDirectory.isEmpty() 'aRootFile'
		return path.filename()
	end 'aRootFile'

	return "{moduleDirectory}{ModuleDirectorySeparator}{path.filename()}"
end 'projectRelativeDisplay'

export typealias LibraryFilePathSet = Set with String

// @generated library-cache begin
module function cacheWriteLibraryFilePathSet(writer LibraryCacheWriter, value LibraryFilePathSet)
	writer.unsigned(value.count() as CacheUnsigned)

	for item in value 'eachItem'
		writer.text(item)
	end 'eachItem'
end 'cacheWriteLibraryFilePathSet'

module function cacheReadLibraryFilePathSet(reader LibraryCacheReader) returns LibraryFilePathSet throws LibraryCacheDamage
	let count = try reader.count()
	var result = LibraryFilePathSet.create()

	for _ in 0 upto count 'eachItem'
		let item = try reader.text()
		result.insert(item)
	end 'eachItem'

	return result
end 'cacheReadLibraryFilePathSet'

// @generated library-cache end

export typealias ModuleDirectoryByFile = Map with (String, String)

// @generated library-cache begin
module function cacheWriteModuleDirectoryByFile(writer LibraryCacheWriter, value ModuleDirectoryByFile)
	writer.unsigned(value.count() as CacheUnsigned)

	for (key, entry) in value 'eachEntry'
		writer.text(key)
		writer.text(entry)
	end 'eachEntry'
end 'cacheWriteModuleDirectoryByFile'

module function cacheReadModuleDirectoryByFile(reader LibraryCacheReader) returns ModuleDirectoryByFile throws LibraryCacheDamage
	let count = try reader.count()
	var result = ModuleDirectoryByFile.create()

	for _ in 0 upto count 'eachEntry'
		let key = try reader.text()
		let entry = try reader.text()
		result.upsert(key, value: entry)
	end 'eachEntry'

	return result
end 'cacheReadModuleDirectoryByFile'

// @generated library-cache end

// The one place a `Visibility` becomes an answer (`ProgramSignatures.isVisibleFrom` asks here too).
// `global` and `public` must answer identically — see `Visibility`.
export function visibleThroughModuleDirectories(directories ModuleDirectoryByFile, visibility Visibility, declPath String, readerPath String) returns bool
	match visibility 'tier'
		global or
			public then return true
		file then return declPath.equals(readerPath)
		module then break 'tier'
	end 'tier'

	if readerPath.equals(CompilerOwnedDeclFilePath.toString()) 'aReaderInNoModule'
		return false
	end 'aReaderInNoModule'

	return moduleDirectoryContains(moduleDirectoryIn(directories, path: declPath), inner: moduleDirectoryIn(directories, path: readerPath))
end 'visibleThroughModuleDirectories'

export function moduleDirectoryIn(directories ModuleDirectoryByFile, path String) returns String
	return try directories.get(path) otherwise panic("moduleDirectoryIn: '{path}' has no module directory — every source path is stamped by `Queries.foldOneFileInto` before any declaration of it is folded")
end 'moduleDirectoryIn'

export typealias UnderlyingContestedAliasSet = Set with ByteArray

// @generated library-cache begin
function cacheWriteUnderlyingContestedAliasSet(writer LibraryCacheWriter, value UnderlyingContestedAliasSet)
	writer.unsigned(value.count() as CacheUnsigned)

	for item in value 'eachItem'
		writer.bytes(item)
	end 'eachItem'
end 'cacheWriteUnderlyingContestedAliasSet'

function cacheReadUnderlyingContestedAliasSet(reader LibraryCacheReader) returns UnderlyingContestedAliasSet throws LibraryCacheDamage
	let count = try reader.count()
	var result = UnderlyingContestedAliasSet.create()

	for _ in 0 upto count 'eachItem'
		let item = try reader.bytes()
		result.insert(item)
	end 'eachItem'

	return result
end 'cacheReadUnderlyingContestedAliasSet'

// @generated library-cache end

export type RangedAliasRegistry
	// Every alias under its BARE name — the fallback, and on its own exactly the pre-existing
	// whole-program behaviour, which is what makes the bare probe safe to keep.
	var byName as RangedTypeAliasMap

	// ⭐ ONLY the names MORE THAN ONE FILE declares, each holding EVERY declaration of that name.
	//
	// **THE INVARIANT, and both halves are load-bearing:** a name is in here IFF at least two files
	// declare it, and when it is, every one of its declarations is (`record` files the incumbent
	// alongside the newcomer at the moment a second file appears). So a MISS is a complete answer —
	// "one file declares this, `byName` has it" — and a HIT never needs a second source.
	//
	// It is what makes a file-scoped lookup free to the names that do not need one: the miss costs
	// one hash of a short type name and no allocation whatever, which is what the whole-program map
	// cost before file scoping existed.
	var multiplyDeclared as MultiplyDeclaredAliasMap

	// ⭐ The WIDEST `rangedAliasStorageBytes` any declaration of a name needs — folded in by `record` as
	// each declaration arrives, so the file-less reader below answers in one probe and never walks.
	//
	// It is a derived INDEX over the same declarations `byName`/`multiplyDeclared` index, maintained by
	// the same single writer, for the same reason `multiplyDeclared` is: the question is asked far more
	// often than it is written. A width is asked once per array op site and once per `sizeof`; it is
	// written once per `typealias` declaration in the program.
	var storageBytes as AliasStorageBytesMap

	// ⭐ The widest `rangedAliasPackedBits` any declaration of a name needs — the index above's twin below
	// the byte boundary, written by the same single writer in the same call, so the two halves of one
	// element's width are never maintained by two rules that can come to cover different declarations.
	var packedBits as AliasPackedBitsMap

	export var provenance as ClaimantProvenance
	var underlyingContested as UnderlyingContestedAliasSet

	// ⭐⭐ **THE NAMES WHOSE DECLARATIONS DISAGREE ABOUT THE RANGE** — a THIRD derived index over the
	// same declarations, and the only one that cannot be maintained by `record`: it is a fact about ALL of a
	// name's declarations at once, so it is settled in one pass after the last one lands
	// (`settleRangeContests`). Empty until then, and empty forever in a program with no contest.
	var rangeContested as RangeContestedAliasMap

	var identities as DeclarationIdentityMap

	// ⭐⭐ **THE BUILTIN `ExitCode`'S HIGH BOUND, RESOLVED FROM THE COMPILE TARGET'S OS ONCE, HERE.**
	// `lookup` is handed a name and a reader's path and nothing else — no project, no target —
	// so the platform fact cannot be derived at the moment it is needed. It is derived at CONSTRUCTION,
	// where `Project.create` and `Queries.resetMergeTargets` and `ProgramSignatures.create` all have a
	// `Target` in hand, and carried on the structure that answers the question.
	//
	// A `let`, mirroring `Project.target`'s, and for its reason: a registry is aimed at one target when it
	// is built and re-aiming it mid-compile would make two lookups of one name disagree.
	let exitCodeHigh as ParsedInt

	// ⚠ **IT TAKES THE TARGET'S `Os`, WHICH IS THE ONE THING A REGISTRY CANNOT LEARN LATER** — see
	// `exitCodeHigh` above. Every construction site supplies it from the `Target` its own caller was
	// aimed with; none of them defaults, because a default at one site is a compile that silently uses the
	// build machine's platform for a cross-compiled binary's `ExitCode`.
	export static function create(os Os) returns RangedAliasRegistry
		let exitCodeHigh = exitCodeAliasHigh(os)
		requireExitCodeRangeFitsItsWidth(exitCodeHigh)
		return Self{byName: RangedTypeAliasMap.create(), multiplyDeclared: MultiplyDeclaredAliasMap.create(), storageBytes: AliasStorageBytesMap.create(), packedBits: AliasPackedBitsMap.create(), provenance: ClaimantProvenance.create(), underlyingContested: UnderlyingContestedAliasSet.create(), rangeContested: sharedEmptyRangeContests, identities: sharedEmptyDeclarationIdentities, exitCodeHigh: exitCodeHigh}
	end 'create'

	export function noteFingerprint(fingerprint IndexFingerprint, group String)
		fingerprint.openGroup(group)
		fingerprint.note("byName", value: self.byName.count())
		fingerprint.note("multiplyDeclared", value: self.multiplyDeclared.count())
		fingerprint.note("storageBytes", value: self.storageBytes.count())
		fingerprint.note("packedBits", value: self.packedBits.count())
		self.provenance.noteFingerprint(fingerprint)
		fingerprint.note("underlyingContested", value: self.underlyingContested.count())
		fingerprint.note("rangeContested", value: self.rangeContested.count())
		fingerprint.note("identities", value: self.identities.count())
		fingerprint.closeGroup()
	end 'noteFingerprint'

	// File one declaration. Declarations of one name over different primitives are legal; they only mark
	// the name `underlyingContested`.
	export function record(alias RangedTypeAlias)
		self.widenElementWidthFor(alias)

		// The FIRST declaration of a name — which, in a program where nothing is redeclared, is every
		// single one of them — collides with nothing and contests nothing. It costs ONE probe and one
		// insert, and never touches `multiplyDeclared` or builds anything at all.
		let sole = try self.byName.get(alias.name) otherwise 'firstDeclarationOfThisName'
			self.byName.upsert(alias.name, value: alias)
			return
		end 'firstDeclarationOfThisName'

		let declPath = alias.declFilePath.toString()

		// EMPTY means no SECOND file has declared this name yet — the invariant puts at least two
		// declarations in every entry that exists — and it doubles as the map to fill if THIS
		// declaration is the one that makes the name contested.
		var byFile = try self.multiplyDeclared.get(alias.name) otherwise RangedAliasByDeclaringFile.create()

		// The two arms below each answer the SAME question — is this newcomer's file already among the
		// files that declare this name — off two different sources, because "the files that declare
		// this name" lives in `byFile` once a name is contested and in `sole` alone before that. What
		// each arm records is that FACT; what the fact MEANS is decided once, after them.
		//
		// A SAME-FILE redeclaration is not this registry's to report (`TypeNameRegistry` owns it, for
		// every declaration kind), but it is still this registry's to CLASSIFY: it must not make the
		// name contested, because a name whose duplicate is already an error has exactly one meaning.
		var declaredByAnotherFile = false

		if byFile.count() > 0 'alreadyContested'
			declaredByAnotherFile = not byFile.contains(declPath)
		end 'alreadyContested' else 'secondDeclarationOfThisName'
			// `sole` is the ONLY other declaration of this name, so it settles the question on its own.
			let solePath = sole.declFilePath.toString()
			declaredByAnotherFile = not solePath.equals(declPath)

			if declaredByAnotherFile 'secondFileDeclaresIt'
				// The incumbent goes in WITH the newcomer: the invariant is "every declaration or
				// none", and an entry holding only the second file's would answer with the WRONG
				// file's range for the first file's own uses — the defect file scoping exists to fix.
				byFile.upsert(solePath, value: sole)
			end 'secondFileDeclaresIt'
		end 'secondDeclarationOfThisName'

		if declaredByAnotherFile and maxonTypeTag(alias.underlying) != maxonTypeTag(sole.underlying) 'underlyingContested'
			self.underlyingContested.insert(alias.name)
		end 'underlyingContested'

		// Non-empty here means the name is contested — either it already was, or the branch above
		// just made it so by filing the incumbent. A same-file redeclaration of a name only one file
		// declares leaves it empty and un-filed, which is right: E3061 is reported against it and the
		// name still has exactly one meaning.
		if byFile.count() > 0 'contested'
			byFile.upsert(declPath, value: alias)
			self.multiplyDeclared.upsert(alias.name, value: byFile)
		end 'contested'

		self.byName.upsert(alias.name, value: alias)
	end 'record'

	function scopedClaimant(byFile RangedAliasByDeclaringFile, readerPath String) returns RangedTypeAlias
		var claims = TypeNameClaimArray.create()

		for (_, claimant) in byFile 'eachClaimant'
			claims.push(self.provenance.claimOf(TypeDeclKind.rangedAlias, declFilePath: claimant.declFilePath, visibility: claimant.visibility))
		end 'eachClaimant'

		return try byFile.get(self.provenance.rankedDeclaringPath(claims, readerPath: readerPath)) otherwise panic("RangedAliasRegistry.scopedClaimant: the ranked declaring file is one of the files these claims were built from")
	end 'scopedClaimant'

	// The alias `name` denotes when read FROM `readerFilePath`.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CONTESTED TEST AND THE SCOPED ANSWER ARE ONE PROBE, and that is the point.** This is
	// asked once per named type reference — the parser's hot path — so nothing here may allocate.
	// A name only one file declares misses `multiplyDeclared` and falls straight through to the bare
	// answer, which is the same answer; a name several files declare is answered out of the very
	// entry that proved it contested, keyed by the reader's path with no composite key to build.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **A FILE THAT NAMES A CONTESTED ALIAS AND DECLARES NONE OF IT RESOLVES THROUGH THE RANKING**
	// (`ClaimantProvenance.rankedClaimIn`), never through the last-wins `byName` door: a declaration
	// the reader may not name ranks below every one it may, and a library file never means an author's
	// declaration, so a user's `typealias Codepoint = int(0 to 100)` never decides what a stdlib
	// function's `Codepoint` accepts. Pinned by `specs/typealias-file-scope.md`'s
	// `third-file-resolves-to-the-nameable-declaration`. Two candidates the reader may both name are
	// E3063's question (`ProgramSignatures.ambiguousAliasCandidates`).
	//
	// ⚠ The extra walk — one pass over that name's claimants, which are the few files declaring it — is
	// paid ONLY by a contested name whose reader declares none of it. An uncontested name, nearly every
	// name in any program, short-circuits on the `multiplyDeclared` miss, so the hot path never reaches it.
	//
	// ⇒ The ranking is ONE body behind every scoped type-name door — this one,
	// `FunctionAliasRegistry.lookup`, `ProgramSignatures.scopedTupleClaimant` and
	// `ProgramSignatures.scopedGenericAliasDecl`. What each door owns is its value type, where its
	// claimants come from and what it falls through to.
	export function lookup(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns DeclaredAlias
		if let byFile = try self.multiplyDeclared.get(name) 'contested'
			if let own = try byFile.get(readerFilePath.toString()) 'readersOwnFile'
				return DeclaredAlias.declared(own)
			end 'readersOwnFile'

			return DeclaredAlias.declared(self.scopedClaimant(byFile, readerPath: readerFilePath.toString()))
		end 'contested'

		if let any = try self.byName.get(name) 'declaredSomewhere'
			return DeclaredAlias.declared(any)
		end 'declaredSomewhere'

		// ⭐⭐ **THE BUILTIN `ExitCode` — LAST, AS A FALLBACK, AND THE POSITION IS THE WHOLE ANSWER TO
		// "WHOSE `ExitCode`?"** It supplies a range for a name NO file declares; where a file DOES
		// declare it, that declaration answers, exactly as it answers for every other alias name.
		//
		// ⛔⛔ **IT MUST NOT COME FIRST: RANGE AND WIDTH MUST NAME ONE DECLARATION.**
		// `storageBytesInEveryFile` holds the SLOT WIDTH a declaration implies, fed by the same `record`,
		// and nothing short-circuits it. Answered first, `typealias ExitCode = int(0 to 125)` + `Array
		// with ExitCode` would have its guard answered by the builtin (`0 to u32.max`, so `300` passes)
		// and its stride by the user (one byte, so `300` is stored as `44`) — a silent wrong answer on
		// every target. A guard NARROWER than its slot is no disagreement: `int(0 to 100)` rides an i64.
		//
		// ⚠ It asks the TABLE (`builtinTypeNameTag`) rather than comparing bytes. Being last, that
		// table's compares are paid only on a lookup that was about to answer `undeclared`, never on the
		// hot path.
		if builtinTypeNameTag(name) == ValueTypeTag.exitCode 'theBuiltin'
			return DeclaredAlias.declared(builtinExitCodeAlias(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath, high: self.exitCodeHigh))
		end 'theBuiltin'

		return DeclaredAlias.undeclared
	end 'lookup'

	// ⭐⭐ **EVERY DECLARATION OF A NAME MORE THAN ONE FILE DECLARES — the set an AMBIGUITY question is
	// decided on.** A name only one file declares answers the SHARED EMPTY map, and that is a
	// COMPLETE answer rather than a cheap one: one declaration is never ambiguous.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT HANDS BACK THE VERY ENTRY `lookup` SHORT-CIRCUITS ON**, so the ambiguity verdict and the
	// resolution it overrides read ONE index and cannot come to disagree about which declarations
	// exist. The verdict itself is `ProgramSignatures.ambiguousAliasCandidates`'.
	export function contestedDeclarations(name ByteArray) returns RangedAliasByDeclaringFile
		return try self.multiplyDeclared.get(name) otherwise sharedEmptyContestedDeclarations
	end 'contestedDeclarations'

	// ⭐ **EVERY NAME SOME FILE DECLARES A RANGED ALIAS UNDER** — for the ONE whole-program walk that has to
	// visit them all (`ProgramSignatures.settleCrossKindContests`, which offers every declared type name to
	// the cross-KIND test). A fresh array rather than the map itself: this registry's index is its own, and
	// handing a caller a live handle to it is how a reader becomes a writer.
	//
	// It allocates once per compile, on a walk that already visits every declaration in the program.
	export function declaredNames() returns ByteArrayArray
		var names = ByteArrayArray.create()

		for (name, _) in self.byName 'eachDeclaredName'
			names.push(name)
		end 'eachDeclaredName'

		return names
	end 'declaredNames'

	export function multiplyDeclaredNames() returns ByteArrayArray
		var names = ByteArrayArray.create()

		for (name, _) in self.multiplyDeclared 'eachMultiplyDeclaredName'
			names.push(name)
		end 'eachMultiplyDeclaredName'

		return names
	end 'multiplyDeclaredNames'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES *ANY* DECLARATION — OR THE BUILTIN — MAKE `name` A RANGED TYPEALIAS?** The
	// allocation-free existence probe the nominal identity door rests on: whether the name denotes a ranged
	// alias at all. Which declaration's identity it carries is `ProgramSignatures.rangedIdentityOfSpelling`'s.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT MUST NOT BOX.** `aggregateNameOf` asks it at every coercion site in the program, and
	// `lookup`'s `DeclaredAlias` is a heap union — one allocation per ask would land on `phase:parse`.
	// Two map/table probes and no allocation whatever.
	export function declaresAlias(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.byName.contains(name) or builtinTypeNameTag(name) == ValueTypeTag.exitCode
	end 'declaresAlias'

	// ⭐ **THE IDENTITY OF A NAME TWO KINDS HOLD.** A `type Foo` and a `typealias Foo` in two files are two
	// types, so the alias half cannot answer to the bare name the nominal half already answers to; it takes
	// the reader's own claimant's range-qualified spelling, which `sourceSpelledAliasName` un-mints for
	// display exactly as a contested element's does. Paid only by a cross-kind contested name.
	export function crossKindIdentityOf(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns ByteArray
		return try self.rangeMintOfReadersDeclaration(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) otherwise EmptySourceText
	end 'crossKindIdentityOf'

	function rangeMintOfReadersDeclaration(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns ByteArray throws AbsentError
		match self.lookup(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'resolved'
			undeclared then throw AbsentError.absent
			declared(alias) then return rangeQualifiedAliasName(name, alias: alias)
		end 'resolved'
	end 'rangeMintOfReadersDeclaration'

	export function fitsTheBuiltinExitCode(alias RangedTypeAlias) returns bool
		return maxonTypeTag(alias.underlying) == ValueTypeTag.integer and not alias.domain.isPattern() and alias.low >= ExitCodeAliasLow and alias.low <= alias.high and alias.high <= self.exitCodeHigh
	end 'fitsTheBuiltinExitCode'

	// ⭐ **THE DECLARATION `byName` HOLDS — no scoping, and NO BUILTIN FALLBACK.** It is deliberately not
	// `lookup`, whose job is to answer what a READER means and which therefore synthesizes the builtin
	// `ExitCode` alias for a name no file declares. A contest is a question about DECLARATIONS, so a
	// synthesized answer here would file a claim for a declaration that appears in no program.
	export function declarationInAnyFile(name ByteArray) returns DeclaredAlias
		if let any = try self.byName.get(name) 'declaredSomewhere'
			return DeclaredAlias.declared(any)
		end 'declaredSomewhere'

		return DeclaredAlias.undeclared
	end 'declarationInAnyFile'

	// ⚠ **THE BARE PROBE, for the readers that HAVE no file to ask from** — type resolution's erasure
	// of a `named` to its primitive (`resolveNamedType`, reached for a `StructLayout` field type,
	// which records no declaring file), conformance-signature canonicalization, union payload
	// classification reached from the emitted runtime (`MmRuntime`/`ManagedMemoryRuntime` walk
	// `project.enumTypes`, which records no declaring file) and generic type-argument canonicalization
	// (`conformerTypeArgName`, which holds an instance id).
	//
	// It is spelled separately, and named for what it does, so that "this site cannot scope its
	// lookup" is greppable rather than hidden behind a path that LOOKS scoped.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT HANDS BACK `underlying` AND NOT THE ALIAS, and that is a CONTRACT, not a convenience.**
	// All four readers want the `int`/`float` keyword and none may read the RANGE — the range is
	// per-file and enforced where the file is known (`InsertRangeChecks`), so a reader with no file
	// reading it would be reading a stranger's bounds. The contract is stated HERE rather than once
	// per reader.
	//
	// ⚠ Two files may declare one file-scoped name over `int` and `float`, and then no file-independent
	// answer exists: a reader that answered anyway would hand the emitter an xmm value in a gpr slot.
	// Such a name is `underlyingContested` and this door panics on it, so a reader that holds a file
	// and asks here instead of `underlyingFor` fails loudly.
	//
	// ⚠ It also costs NOTHING, where a `DeclaredAlias` answer cost one allocation per call: a union
	// with an associated value is HEAP-BOXED, and `resolveNamedType` asks this once per named TYPE
	// REFERENCE. Measured on the scale ladder, that box was +36 x 2^n allocations — a term that grew
	// with the PROGRAM, where every other cost of alias resolution grows only with the DECLARATIONS.
	// Returning a field that already holds a `MaxonType` constructs nothing. The `throws` channel is
	// `Map.get`'s own and is likewise free.
	export function underlyingInAnyFile(name ByteArray) returns MaxonType throws MapError
		let any = try self.byName.get(name)

		if self.underlyingContested.contains(name) 'itsFilesDisagreeAboutThePrimitive'
			panic("RangedAliasRegistry.underlyingInAnyFile: '{String.from(name)}' is declared over different underlying types by different files, so only a reader's own file can answer — this reader must ask `underlyingFor`")
		end 'itsFilesDisagreeAboutThePrimitive'

		return any.underlying
	end 'underlyingInAnyFile'

	export function underlyingFor(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns MaxonType throws MapError
		if not self.byName.contains(name) 'noFileDeclaresIt'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'noFileDeclaresIt'

		match self.lookup(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'resolved'
			undeclared then panic("RangedAliasRegistry.underlyingFor: '{String.from(name)}' is declared by some file and resolves to no declaration from '{readerFilePath.toString()}'")
			declared(alias) then return alias.underlying
		end 'resolved'
	end 'underlyingFor'

	// The range as SOURCE spells it -- `int(0 to 255)`. A name whose declarations disagree about the range, or
	// whose declarations are each their own type, has no one range to be shown as, so it is refused.
	export function uncontestedRangeSourceSpelling(name ByteArray) returns ByteArray throws MapError
		if self.rangeContested.contains(name) or self.nameTakesAnIdentity(name) 'itsDeclarationsDisagreeAboutTheRange'
			throw MapError.keyNotFound
		end 'itsDeclarationsDisagreeAboutTheRange'

		let any = try self.byName.get(name)
		return rangeSourceSpelling(any.underlying, low: any.low, high: any.high)
	end 'uncontestedRangeSourceSpelling'

	// Does SOME file declare `name` as a ranged typealias — the membership half of `underlyingInAnyFile`,
	// over the identical table, so the two cannot come to disagree about what this registry holds. It is the
	// FORM question (`ProgramSignatures.declaredFormOf`) and deliberately not the bounds one: which file's
	// bounds a reader gets is `lookup`'s answer and needs a reader file, while "is this name a ranged alias
	// at all" is the same for every reader.
	//
	// ⚠ It does NOT answer for the builtin `ExitCode`, exactly as `underlyingInAnyFile` does not: the
	// builtin range is `lookup`'s LAST fallback, supplied for the name no file declares. A form derived
	// from it would report a `typealias` no program contains.
	export function containsInAnyFile(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.byName.contains(name)
	end 'containsInAnyFile'

	export function declaredAsFloatInAnyFile(name ByteArray) returns bool
		let any = try self.byName.get(name) otherwise return false
		return tagIsFloat(maxonTypeTag(any.underlying)) or self.underlyingContested.contains(name)
	end 'declaredAsFloatInAnyFile'

	// ⭐ **THE STORAGE WIDTH THAT HOLDS EVERY FILE'S DECLARATION OF `name` — the other file-less door, and
	// the one range question a file-less reader IS allowed to ask.**
	//
	// `underlyingInAnyFile` above refuses to hand out bounds, and its reason is exact: a reader with no
	// file reading A file's bounds is reading a STRANGER's bounds. That reason does not reach this
	// question, because this answer is not any one file's — it is a width correct for every file's bounds
	// at once, and it is the MAX that makes it so. A wider slot holds a narrower one's values, so no
	// file's range is ever truncated by another file's width.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE FOLD IS NOT OPTIONAL, because a generic instance is interned on the NAME.**
	// `Array with Small` is ONE instance however many files declare `Small`, so its `element_size@24` is
	// ONE number — a record created in one file is pushed, appended, read and dropped in another, and they
	// must stride identically. A bare `byName` probe would answer with whichever file merged LAST: two
	// files declaring one name as `int(1 to 2)` and `int(0 to u64.max)` would stride 1 or 8 by
	// load order, and on the 1 the file that meant 8 would truncate silently — the order-dependent wrong
	// answer file scoping exists to kill, one door along.
	//
	// A name only ONE file declares — nearly every name — cost exactly one probe to fold and costs exactly
	// one probe to read. `throws` is `Map.get`'s own channel: a name no file declares is not a ranged
	// alias, which is the caller's case to handle, not a width to invent.
	export function storageBytesInEveryFile(name ByteArray) returns ByteOffset throws MapError
		return try self.storageBytes.get(name)
	end 'storageBytesInEveryFile'

	// ⭐ **THE SUB-BYTE PACKED WIDTH THAT HOLDS EVERY FILE'S DECLARATION OF `name`** — the door above's
	// twin below the byte boundary, and file-less for the identical reason: a generic instance is interned
	// on the NAME, so `Array with Q` is ONE instance with ONE `element_size@24` however many files declare
	// `Q`, and an answer that varied by reader would let one file's record stride 2 bits while another's
	// strode a byte.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT DOES NOT THROW, AND THE MISS IS AN ANSWER RATHER THAN A GAP.** A name no file declares as a
	// ranged alias is an enum, a boxed union's ordinal, or the `Byte` a `b"…"` literal mints for itself —
	// and NOT ONE OF THEM PACKS. So `byteStrided` is complete and correct for the whole miss arm,
	// where the width door has to throw because its miss splits two ways
	// (`ProgramSignatures.undeclaredNamedElementSlot`).
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THAT IS ALSO WHERE ENUMS ARE EXCLUDED, AND IT COSTS NOTHING TO STATE.** Enums are not in
	// this registry at all, so they miss here and the exclusion is structural rather than a rule
	// someone has to remember to keep. `enum-narrow-storage` forbids packing a 3-case enum into 2 bits.
	export function packedBitsInEveryFile(name ByteArray) returns ElementPacking
		return try self.packedBits.get(name) otherwise ElementPacking.byteStrided
	end 'packedBitsInEveryFile'

	// ⭐ **DOES ANY FILE DECLARE `name` AS A RANGED ALIAS AT ALL?** The door above's `throws` channel
	// as a plain bool, for the one reader that wants whether a declaration EXISTS and never what it
	// says: `ProgramSignatures.elementIsAByteNoFileDeclares` is asking whether an element's slot comes
	// from `undeclaredNamedElementSlot` — the miss arm — and therefore has no declared range for a raw
	// byte to fall outside of. It reads the SAME index for the same reason `storageBytesInEveryFile`
	// does: `widenElementWidthFor` is the only entry that writes it and runs on every declaration
	// before any branching, so "present here" and "some file declares it" are one fact.
	export function anyFileDeclares(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.storageBytes.contains(name)
	end 'anyFileDeclares'

	// Fold one declaration's width into the running max for its name — BOTH halves of it, above and below
	// the byte boundary. The ONLY writer of `storageBytes` and of `packedBits`, called by `record` before
	// any of its own branching, so both indices cover every declaration whatever that branching concludes
	// about contest or conflict.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE TWO FOLDS ARE ONE CALL BECAUSE THEY ARE ONE FACT.** A name's element width is a single answer
	// that happens to be spelled in bytes above the boundary and in bits below it; folding one here and the
	// other anywhere else would be two writers over the same declarations, free to cover different ones.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE FIRST DECLARATION IS WRITTEN IN ONE PLACE FOR BOTH INDICES, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES THEM
	// LOCKSTEP.** `packedBits` has an entry for exactly the names `storageBytes` has one for, because this
	// is the only writer of either and it seeds both on the same event. The two `widen*` helpers below are
	// therefore only ever asked about a name that is already present — which is why each may `panic` on a
	// miss rather than quietly seeding a second time, and why they read as the two halves of one fold
	// instead of one inline arm and one helper.
	function widenElementWidthFor(alias RangedTypeAlias)
		if not self.storageBytes.contains(alias.name) 'firstDeclarationOfThisName'
			self.storageBytes.upsert(alias.name, value: rangedAliasStorageBytes(alias.low, high: alias.high))
			self.packedBits.upsert(alias.name, value: rangedAliasPackedBits(alias.low, high: alias.high))
			return
		end 'firstDeclarationOfThisName'

		self.widenStorageBytesFor(alias)
		self.widenPackedBitsFor(alias)
	end 'widenElementWidthFor'

	// The BYTE half of the fold, for the second and later declarations of a name: the widest slot any of
	// them needs, so no file's range is ever truncated by another file's width.
	function widenStorageBytesFor(alias RangedTypeAlias)
		let width = rangedAliasStorageBytes(alias.low, high: alias.high)
		let widest = try self.storageBytes.get(alias.name) otherwise panic("RangedAliasRegistry.widenStorageBytesFor: '{String.from(alias.name)}' has a later declaration and no first one — `widenElementWidthFor` seeds both indices together, so a miss here means something else wrote one of them")

		if width > widest 'wider'
			self.storageBytes.upsert(alias.name, value: width)
		end 'wider'
	end 'widenStorageBytesFor'

	// The PACKED half of the fold, for the second and later declarations of a name.
	//
	// ⚠ **A BYTE BEATS EVERY PACKED WIDTH, WHICH IS WHY `byteStrided` IS ABSORBING RATHER THAN
	// SMALLEST.** It is the same "widest wins" rule the byte fold above applies, read on the correct
	// ladder: a byte-strided slot holds every value a 4-bit field holds, so a name one file declares
	// `int(0 to 3)` and another `int(0 to 100)` must stride a BYTE, or the file that meant 100 is
	// truncated. Reached only when a name has a second declaration, which is nearly no name.
	function widenPackedBitsFor(alias RangedTypeAlias)
		let bits = rangedAliasPackedBits(alias.low, high: alias.high)
		let sofar = try self.packedBits.get(alias.name) otherwise panic("RangedAliasRegistry.widenPackedBitsFor: '{String.from(alias.name)}' has a byte width and no packed one — the two indices are seeded together by `widenElementWidthFor` and cannot disagree about which names they hold")

		let sofarWidth = try packedWidthOf(sofar) otherwise return

		if let width = try packedWidthOf(bits) 'packed'
			if width > sofarWidth 'wider'
				self.packedBits.upsert(alias.name, value: bits)
			end 'wider'
		end 'packed' else 'aByteStride'
			self.packedBits.upsert(alias.name, value: bits)
		end 'aByteStride'
	end 'widenPackedBitsFor'

	// ⭐⭐ **IS `name` A PURE SECOND NAME FOR `int` — the WHOLE i64 span, whichever file's declaration you
	// read?** The `holdsEveryByte` twin one bar up, and its reader is the inferred-int-element boundary
	// (`ProgramSignatures.inferredIntColumnBoundaryAdmits`): an array a bare `[10, 20, 30]` built carries the
	// compiler's own `integer` element, and it may cross into a declared `Array with <alias>` exactly when
	// that alias adds NOTHING to `int` — no narrower value set to check, and (a consequence, not a second
	// condition) no narrower slot, since `rangedAliasStorageBytes` gives the full span the machine word.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE BAR IS BOTH ENDS EXACTLY, NOT "WIDE ENOUGH".** A range is compared as the RAW 64-bit pattern
	// (`RangedTypeAlias`'s header), so a `high` above `i64.max` is indistinguishable from a negative one —
	// `typealias Big = int(0 to u64.max)` would read as spanning everything while refusing every negative
	// element a literal may hold. Demanding the exact pair is the only reading those bits support, and it is
	// also the only one under which the crossing is observationally free.
	//
	// ⚠ Asked only after two aggregate names have already DISAGREED — a rejected program's path — so the walk
	// over one name's declarations costs every compiling program nothing, exactly as its twin does.
	export function spansEveryIntInEveryFile(name ByteArray) returns bool throws MapError
		if let byFile = try self.multiplyDeclared.get(name) 'declaredByMoreThanOneFile'
			for (_, alias) in byFile 'eachDeclaringFile'
				if not rangeSpansEveryInt(alias.low, high: alias.high) 'thisFilesDeclarationDoesNot'
					return false
				end 'thisFilesDeclarationDoesNot'
			end 'eachDeclaringFile'

			return true
		end 'declaredByMoreThanOneFile'

		let sole = try self.byName.get(name)
		return rangeSpansEveryInt(sole.low, high: sole.high)
	end 'spansEveryIntInEveryFile'

	// ⭐⭐ **CAN A VALUE OF `name` HOLD EVERY BYTE — 0 THROUGH 255 — WHICHEVER FILE'S DECLARATION YOU
	// READ?** The THIRD file-less door, and it is one for the SAME reason `storageBytesInEveryFile`
	// is: the answer is not any one file's. Where that door folds to the WIDEST width so no file's
	// range is truncated, this one demands the property of EVERY declaration, so no file's range is
	// over-promised. `underlyingInAnyFile`'s refusal to hand out bounds is untouched — a reader with
	// no file may not learn what SOME file's `low` and `high` are, and this reader never does; it
	// learns only whether they all clear one fixed bar.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE BAR IS THE VALUE SET AND NOT THE WIDTH, AND THE TWO ARE A WRONG ANSWER APART.**
	// `rangedAliasStorageBytes` gives EVERY non-negative range that fits `u8.max` a ONE-BYTE slot, so
	// `int(0 to 200)` strides one byte and cannot hold `223`. Its only reader is the byte-element
	// boundary (`ProgramSignatures.byteBufferBoundaryAdmits`), where a compiler-synthesized buffer — a
	// run of RAW bytes — is admitted into a declared `Array with Byte`: what receives one must admit
	// every value a byte has, and a slot that merely fits in one byte is not that.
	//
	// ⚠ It is asked only after two aggregate names have already DISAGREED, which is a rejected
	// program's path, so the walk over one name's declarations costs every compiling program nothing.
	//
	// `throws` is `Map.get`'s own channel: a name NO file declares is not a ranged alias at all, and
	// what the compiler's own two byte elements are worth is the caller's to state, not this index's
	// to invent.
	export function holdsEveryByteInEveryFile(name ByteArray) returns bool throws MapError
		if let byFile = try self.multiplyDeclared.get(name) 'declaredByMoreThanOneFile'
			for (_, alias) in byFile 'eachDeclaringFile'
				if not rangeHoldsEveryByte(alias.low, high: alias.high) 'thisFilesDeclarationCannot'
					return false
				end 'thisFilesDeclarationCannot'
			end 'eachDeclaringFile'

			return true
		end 'declaredByMoreThanOneFile'

		let sole = try self.byName.get(name)
		return rangeHoldsEveryByte(sole.low, high: sole.high)
	end 'holdsEveryByteInEveryFile'

	// ⭐⭐ **DECIDE WHICH NAMES ARE RANGE-CONTESTED, AND MINT THE QUALIFIED DECLARATION EACH DISTINCT RANGE
	// GETS** — the answer the door above cannot give, made once, after every declaration is in.
	//
	// `widenStorageBytesFor`'s header states the constraint this settles: a generic instance is
	// interned on the element's NAME, so `Array with Byte` is ONE instance with ONE `element_size@24`
	// however many files declare `Byte`, and no width rule lets a library buffer built through it and
	// a user's wider `Byte` share it. **The two `Array with Byte` must become two INSTANCES.**
	//
	// ⇒ a name whose declarations disagree about the range gets one MINTED declaration per distinct range —
	// `Byte$0_255` beside `Byte$0_1000` — and an instance's element is spelled with the mint its READER
	// resolves to (`rangeScopedName`). Where an author's declaration takes part, each declaration is its
	// own type and gets its own mint, two over one range included (`mintsOfDeclarations`); the range walk
	// skips such a name.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE MINT IS AN ORDINARY REGISTRY DECLARATION, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN.** Every door this
	// registry already has then answers for the qualified name with no new code and no special case: the
	// whole-program width (`storageBytesInEveryFile`), the erasure (`underlyingInAnyFile`), the scoped
	// `lookup` a range check resolves through. A bare synthesized STRING would miss all of them, and
	// `undeclaredNamedElementSlot` would stride the element at 8.
	//
	// ⚠ **A NAME ONLY ONE FILE DECLARES CANNOT REACH THE RANGE WALK**, because `multiplyDeclared`
	// holds only the names two or more files declare — and a name every declaration of which agrees
	// about the range falls out on the `< 2` test. `int(0 to 255)` and `int(0 to u8.max)` are one
	// claimant: `u8.max` is resolved to its value before anything is compared.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE CONTEST IS COLLECTED BEFORE ANY MINT IS RECORDED**, because `record` writes into the very map
	// this walks.
	//
	// ⚠ **AND NOT ONE NAME IS SPELLED UNTIL A CONTEST IS PROVEN.** The dedup compares the BOUNDS;
	// spelling each declaration to compare STRINGS would allocate a `ByteArray` per declaration of
	// every multiply-declared name, every one of them thrown away in a program that contests nothing.
	//
	// ⚠⚠ **THE CONTEST MAPS ARE PUBLISHED LAST, AFTER THE MINTS THEY GUARD ARE IN THE REGISTRY.**
	// `anyRangeContest()` — hence `fileScopedInstance`, hence every qualified element name in the
	// compiler — is exactly the test that one of the two published maps is non-empty. The moment that
	// answers true, a reader may ask for `Byte$0_1000`, and a `Byte$0_1000` the registry does not hold
	// answers the WIDTH FALLBACK (`undeclaredNamedElementSlot`), which — since `isByteElementName` asks
	// about the BASE name — hands a 2-byte element a ONE-BYTE stride with no diagnostic. Building into a
	// LOCAL and assigning at the end makes "the mints exist before anything can name them" a property of
	// the code rather than of the fact that nothing currently runs between the loops.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **`alsoScope` IS EVERY NAME A SECOND *KIND* OF DECLARATION ALSO HOLDS** — the cross-KIND contest's
	// names (`ProgramSignatures.crossKindScopedAliasNames`), scoped through exactly this mechanism and not a
	// second one. A `type ParsedInt` in a user file and a `typealias ParsedInt` in `stdlib/Builtins.maxon`
	// give the name two MEANINGS rather than two ranges, but the thing that has to happen to a container
	// over it is identical: its element needs a spelling only ONE declaration answers for, so that a reader
	// with no file — the whole managed tier (`typeIsManaged`, `managedFieldDropCallee`) — cannot resolve it
	// to the other one, and drop an array of integers through the struct's destructor.
	//
	// ⚠ **SUCH A NAME IS SCOPED EVEN WITH ONE RANGE CLAIMANT, which is the one rule the two halves do not
	// share.** A range contest needs two claimants by definition — one range is no disagreement — while a
	// KIND contest's alias side is very often a single declaration, and it is precisely that declaration the
	// container's element must be pinned to. The `< 2` test therefore belongs to the range walk alone.
	export function settleRangeContests(alsoScope ByteArrayArray)
		var mints = RangedTypeAliasArray.create()
		var declarationIdentities = DeclarationIdentityMap.create()

		for (name, _) in self.multiplyDeclared 'eachNameWhoseDeclarationsMayMeet'
			let claimants = self.declarationClaimants(name)

			if not self.nameTakesDeclarationIdentities(claimants) 'noReaderCanNameTwoOfThem'
				continue
			end 'noReaderCanNameTwoOfThem'

			declarationIdentities.upsert(name, value: mintsOfDeclarations(name, claimants: claimants, mints: mints, provenance: self.provenance))
		end 'eachNameWhoseDeclarationsMayMeet'

		// ⚠ **BUILT OWNED, AND THE SHARED EMPTY HANDLE IS WHAT THE FIELD KEEPS WHEN NOTHING IS CONTESTED.**
		// Starting AS `sharedEmptyRangeContests` and copying on write at the first contest, guarded by an
		// `is`-identity test, is a mutable name reaching a module-level `let`'s record, which single
		// ownership refuses (E3019 at the `upsert`) — the guard that would make it safe is invisible to the
		// rule, and the rule is about what the name CAN reach. The publish below is gated on `count() > 0`,
		// so an uncontested program never touches the field; the cost is one empty map.
		var settled = RangeContestedAliasMap.create()

		for (name, _) in self.multiplyDeclared 'eachMultiplyDeclaredName'
			if declarationIdentities.contains(name) 'itsDeclarationsAreEachTheirOwnType'
				continue
			end 'itsDeclarationsAreEachTheirOwnType'

			let claimants = self.distinctRangeClaimants(name)

			if claimants.count() < 2 'everyDeclarationAgreesAboutTheRange'
				continue
			end 'everyDeclarationAgreesAboutTheRange'

			settled.upsert(name, value: scopedSpellingsOf(name, claimants: claimants, mints: mints))
		end 'eachMultiplyDeclaredName'

		// ⭐ The cross-KIND half — see this function's header. It runs SECOND and skips a name the range walk
		// already scoped, so a name that is both range-contested AND kind-contested keeps the range walk's
		// spellings: those are one per RANGE, which is the finer split of the two and subsumes this one.
		for name in alsoScope 'eachKindContestedName'
			if settled.contains(name) or declarationIdentities.contains(name) 'theRangeContestAlreadyScopedIt'
				continue
			end 'theRangeContestAlreadyScopedIt'

			let kindClaimants = self.distinctRangeClaimants(name)

			if kindClaimants.count() == 0 'noRangedDeclarationHoldsIt'
				continue
			end 'noRangedDeclarationHoldsIt'

			settled.upsert(name, value: scopedSpellingsOf(name, claimants: kindClaimants, mints: mints))
		end 'eachKindContestedName'

		// ⚠ RECORDED BEFORE THE PUBLISH BELOW, and separately from the walk above because `record` writes
		// `multiplyDeclared` — the very map that walk iterates.
		for mint in mints 'eachMint'
			self.record(mint)
		end 'eachMint'

		// The PUBLISH. Guarded on `count()` so an uncontested program never reassigns the field at all — it
		// keeps the shared handle it was born with, rather than being handed the same handle a second time.
		if settled.count() > 0 'someNameIsContested'
			self.rangeContested = settled
		end 'someNameIsContested'

		if declarationIdentities.count() > 0 'someNamesDeclarationsMeet'
			self.identities = declarationIdentities
		end 'someNamesDeclarationsMeet'
	end 'settleRangeContests'

	export function namesAContestedRange(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.rangeContested.contains(name) or self.identities.contains(name)
	end 'namesAContestedRange'

	// ⭐ **DOES ANY NAME IN THIS PROGRAM HAVE DECLARATIONS THAT DISAGREE ABOUT THE RANGE, OR THAT ARE EACH
	// THEIR OWN TYPE?** The ONE bool every scoping door short-circuits on, so an uncontested program — the
	// whole corpus, and the compiler's own source — pays a map-emptiness test and nothing else, and no
	// interned element name and no mangled symbol can move.
	export function anyRangeContest() returns bool
		return self.rangeContested.count() > 0 or self.identities.count() > 0
	end 'anyRangeContest'

	// ⭐⭐ **EVERY DISTINCT READING ENVIRONMENT A RANGE-CONTESTED PROGRAM HAS** — one path per file that
	// declares a contested name, plus the EMPTY path, which stands for a file that declares none of
	// them.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT EXISTS SO THE PARSE CAN STAY A PURE READ.** A file's parse may not be the first to intern an
	// instance — the shared registry is written by the sweep and by nothing that fans out — so every
	// (instance, environment) pair a parse could ask for is interned in one pass beforehand
	// (`ProgramSignatures.settleRangeScopedInstances`), and this is the list that makes that pass COMPLETE:
	// a reader's answer is one of these declarations or the stranger's, and there is no third case.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT READS `distinctRangeClaimants`, NOT `contestedDeclarations`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS THE WHOLE
	// CROSS-KIND HALF.** A name only one file declares as a ranged alias holds NO entry in
	// `multiplyDeclared` — so asking that map directly would yield no environment at all for a
	// KIND-contested name, and the one file whose container has to be scoped (`stdlib/Builtins.maxon`)
	// would never be an environment. The claimant walk answers for both halves off one rule.
	export function rangeContestEnvironments() returns FilePathArray
		var environments = FilePathArray.create()
		var seen = DeclaringFilePathSet.create()
		environments.push(CompilerOwnedDeclFilePath)
		seen.insert(CompilerOwnedDeclFilePath.toString())

		for (name, _) in self.rangeContested 'eachContestedName'
			for claimant in self.distinctRangeClaimants(name) 'eachDeclaration'
				admitEnvironment(environments, seen: seen, path: claimant.declFilePath)
			end 'eachDeclaration'
		end 'eachContestedName'

		for (name, _) in self.identities 'eachNameWithADeclarationIdentity'
			for claimant in self.declarationClaimants(name) 'eachDeclarationOfIt'
				admitEnvironment(environments, seen: seen, path: claimant.declFilePath)
			end 'eachDeclarationOfIt'
		end 'eachNameWithADeclarationIdentity'

		return environments
	end 'rangeContestEnvironments'

	// ⭐ **ONE DECLARATION PER DISTINCT RANGE SOME FILE GIVES `name`** — the set both halves of
	// `settleRangeContests` mint from, and the set `rangeContestEnvironments` reads its files off, so
	// "which declarations does a contested name have" is answered in one place.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE SOLE-DECLARATION FALLBACK IS WHAT MAKES THE CROSS-KIND HALF POSSIBLE.** `multiplyDeclared`
	// holds only the names two or more FILES declare; a name one file declares as a ranged alias while
	// another holds it as a `type` is not in it, and that pair is exactly the one the cross-kind half exists
	// for. It is `byName` and not `lookup`: this asks about DECLARATIONS, and `lookup` synthesizes the
	// builtin `ExitCode` for a name no file declares at all.
	function distinctRangeClaimants(name ByteArray) returns RangedTypeAliasArray
		var claimants = RangedTypeAliasArray.create()

		let byFile = self.contestedDeclarations(name)

		for (_, alias) in byFile 'eachDeclaringFile'
			if not holdsRange(claimants, alias: alias) 'aRangeNoEarlierDeclarationClaimed'
				claimants.push(alias)
			end 'aRangeNoEarlierDeclarationClaimed'
		end 'eachDeclaringFile'

		if claimants.count() > 1 'theDeclarationsDisagree'
			return self.rangeRepresentativesInFoldOrder(byFile)
		end 'theDeclarationsDisagree'

		if claimants.count() > 0 'severalFilesDeclareIt'
			return claimants
		end 'severalFilesDeclareIt'

		if let sole = try self.byName.get(name) 'oneFileDeclaresIt'
			claimants.push(sole)
		end 'oneFileDeclaresIt'

		return claimants
	end 'distinctRangeClaimants'

	// One declaration per distinct range, the earliest-folding of each, in fold order — so a contest's
	// mints, their declaring files and their order never depend on a map's iteration order.
	function rangeRepresentativesInFoldOrder(byFile RangedAliasByDeclaringFile) returns RangedTypeAliasArray
		var representatives = RangedTypeAliasArray.create()

		for (_, alias) in byFile 'eachDeclaringFile'
			self.admitRangeRepresentative(representatives, alias: alias)
		end 'eachDeclaringFile'

		return self.inFoldOrder(representatives)
	end 'rangeRepresentativesInFoldOrder'

	function inFoldOrder(aliases RangedTypeAliasArray) returns RangedTypeAliasArray
		var ranked = FoldRankedAliasArray.create()

		for alias in aliases 'eachAlias'
			let declPath = alias.declFilePath.toString()
			ranked.push(FoldRankedAlias.create(self.provenance.foldPositionOf(declPath), alias: alias))
		end 'eachAlias'

		ranked.sort(function(left FoldRankedAlias, right FoldRankedAlias) gives foldRankOrdering(left, right: right))
		var ordered = RangedTypeAliasArray.create()

		for entry in ranked 'eachRanked'
			ordered.push(entry.alias)
		end 'eachRanked'

		return ordered
	end 'inFoldOrder'

	function admitRangeRepresentative(representatives RangedTypeAliasArray, alias RangedTypeAlias)
		for at in 0 upto representatives.count() 'eachHeldRange'
			let held = try representatives.get(at) otherwise panic("RangedAliasRegistry.admitRangeRepresentative: get OOB at {at} inside its own count()")

			if held.hasSameRangeAs(alias) 'theSameRange'
				if self.provenance.foldsLater(held.declFilePath.toString(), thanPath: alias.declFilePath.toString()) 'foldsEarlierThanTheHeldOne'
					try representatives.set(at, value: alias) otherwise panic("RangedAliasRegistry.admitRangeRepresentative: set OOB at {at} inside its own count()")
				end 'foldsEarlierThanTheHeldOne'

				return
			end 'theSameRange'
		end 'eachHeldRange'

		representatives.push(alias)
	end 'admitRangeRepresentative'

	export function anyIdentityContest() returns bool
		return self.identities.count() > 0
	end 'anyIdentityContest'

	export function hasIdentityContest(name ByteArray) returns bool
		return self.identities.contains(name)
	end 'hasIdentityContest'

	export function nameTakesAnIdentity(name ByteArray) returns bool
		let base = sourceSpelledAliasName(name)
		let byFile = try self.identities.get(base) otherwise return false

		if not isMintName(name) 'aBareName'
			return true
		end 'aBareName'

		for (_, identity) in byFile 'eachIdentity'
			if identity.equals(name) 'oneOfItsIdentities'
				return true
			end 'oneOfItsIdentities'
		end 'eachIdentity'

		return false
	end 'nameTakesAnIdentity'

	export function identityOfDeclaration(name ByteArray, alias RangedTypeAlias) returns ByteArray
		let byFile = try self.identities.get(name) otherwise panic("RangedAliasRegistry.identityOfDeclaration: '{String.from(name)}' has no declaration identities, and only a name whose declarations meet has one apiece")
		return try byFile.get(alias.declFilePath.toString()) otherwise panic("RangedAliasRegistry.identityOfDeclaration: '{String.from(name)}' resolved to a declaration in '{alias.declFilePath.toString()}', which holds no identity — `settleRangeContests` gives every declaring file of a contested name one")
	end 'identityOfDeclaration'

	export function identityOfName(base ByteArray, name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns ByteArray
		if not isMintName(name) 'aBareName'
			match self.lookup(base, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) 'resolved'
				undeclared then panic("RangedAliasRegistry.identityOfName: '{String.from(base)}' is contested and resolves to no declaration from '{readerFilePath.toString()}'")
				declared(alias) then return self.identityOfDeclaration(base, alias: alias)
			end 'resolved'
		end 'aBareName'

		let byFile = try self.identities.get(base) otherwise panic("RangedAliasRegistry.identityOfName: '{String.from(base)}' has no declaration identities")

		for (_, identity) in byFile 'eachIdentity'
			if identity.equals(name) 'alreadyAnIdentity'
				return name
			end 'alreadyAnIdentity'
		end 'eachIdentity'

		panic("RangedAliasRegistry.identityOfName: '{String.from(name)}' is no declaration's identity of '{String.from(base)}'")
	end 'identityOfName'

	function declarationClaimants(name ByteArray) returns RangedTypeAliasArray
		var claimants = RangedTypeAliasArray.create()
		let byFile = self.contestedDeclarations(name)

		if byFile.count() == 0 'oneFileDeclaresIt'
			if let sole = try self.byName.get(name) 'theSoleDeclaration'
				claimants.push(sole)
			end 'theSoleDeclaration'

			return claimants
		end 'oneFileDeclaresIt'

		for (_, alias) in byFile 'eachDeclaringFile'
			claimants.push(alias)
		end 'eachDeclaringFile'

		return self.inFoldOrder(claimants)
	end 'declarationClaimants'

	function nameTakesDeclarationIdentities(claimants RangedTypeAliasArray) returns bool
		if claimants.count() < 2 'oneDeclarationIsNeverContested'
			return false
		end 'oneDeclarationIsNeverContested'

		var declPaths = StringArray.create()

		for held in claimants 'eachDeclaration'
			declPaths.push(held.declFilePath.toString())
		end 'eachDeclaration'

		return self.provenance.anAuthorDeclaresAny(declPaths)
	end 'nameTakesDeclarationIdentities'

	// ⭐⭐ **WHAT `name` IS CALLED AS A GENERIC-INSTANCE ELEMENT WHEN READ FROM `readerFilePath`** — the
	// bare name for every name in an uncontested program, and the reader's OWN claimant's mint for a
	// contested one.
	//
	// ⭐ **IT RESOLVES THROUGH `lookup`, NOT THROUGH A SECOND RULE.** Which declaration a file means is
	// decided in exactly one place. Deriving the element spelling from that answer is what keeps
	// "which `Byte` does this file mean?" and "which `Array with Byte` does it build?" one question.
	export function rangeScopedName(name ByteArray, readerFilePath FilePath) returns ByteArray
		if self.nameTakesAnIdentity(name) 'everyDeclarationIsItsOwnElement'
			return self.identityOfName(sourceSpelledAliasName(name), name: name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath)
		end 'everyDeclarationIsItsOwnElement'

		if not self.rangeContested.contains(name) 'everyDeclarationAgreesAboutTheRange'
			return name
		end 'everyDeclarationAgreesAboutTheRange'

		return try self.rangeMintOfReadersDeclaration(name, readerFilePath: readerFilePath) otherwise panic("RangedAliasRegistry.rangeScopedName: '{String.from(name)}' is range-contested but resolves to no declaration from '{readerFilePath.toString()}' — `rangeContested` is derived from `multiplyDeclared`, whose every entry is a declaration `lookup` can find")
	end 'rangeScopedName'

	// @generated library-cache begin
	module function cacheWrite(writer LibraryCacheWriter)
		cacheWriteRangedTypeAliasMap(writer, value: self.byName)
		cacheWriteMultiplyDeclaredAliasMap(writer, value: self.multiplyDeclared)
		cacheWriteAliasStorageBytesMap(writer, value: self.storageBytes)
		cacheWriteAliasPackedBitsMap(writer, value: self.packedBits)
		self.provenance.cacheWrite(writer)
		cacheWriteUnderlyingContestedAliasSet(writer, value: self.underlyingContested)
		cacheWriteRangeContestedAliasMap(writer, value: self.rangeContested)
		cacheWriteDeclarationIdentityMap(writer, value: self.identities)
		writer.signed(self.exitCodeHigh as CacheSigned)
	end 'cacheWrite'

	module static function cacheRead(reader LibraryCacheReader) returns RangedAliasRegistry throws LibraryCacheDamage
		let f_byName = try cacheReadRangedTypeAliasMap(reader)
		let f_multiplyDeclared = try cacheReadMultiplyDeclaredAliasMap(reader)
		let f_storageBytes = try cacheReadAliasStorageBytesMap(reader)
		let f_packedBits = try cacheReadAliasPackedBitsMap(reader)
		let f_provenance = try ClaimantProvenance.cacheRead(reader)
		let f_underlyingContested = try cacheReadUnderlyingContestedAliasSet(reader)
		let f_rangeContested = try cacheReadRangeContestedAliasMap(reader)
		let f_identities = try cacheReadDeclarationIdentityMap(reader)
		let f_exitCodeHigh = (try reader.signed()) as ParsedInt

		return Self{byName: f_byName, multiplyDeclared: f_multiplyDeclared, storageBytes: f_storageBytes, packedBits: f_packedBits, provenance: f_provenance, underlyingContested: f_underlyingContested, rangeContested: f_rangeContested, identities: f_identities, exitCodeHigh: f_exitCodeHigh}
	end 'cacheRead'

	static function cacheLayoutBytes() returns CacheSize72
		return sizeof(RangedAliasRegistry) as CacheSize72
	end 'cacheLayoutBytes'

	// @generated library-cache end
end 'RangedAliasRegistry'

// The qualified SPELLING of each claimant, with the declaration each spelling names pushed onto `mints`.
// Both halves of `settleRangeContests` build their entry through it, so a range-scoped element and a
// kind-scoped one are spelled and registered by one act — a second speller would be a mint no reader can
// find (see that function's ⚠⚠ note on publishing the map last).
//
// ⚠ **ITS MINTS ARE `public`, WHERE THE CLAIMANT MAY BE `file`.** A range mint is compiler-supplied,
// exactly like the builtin `ExitCode`, and its NAME is unspellable in source
// (`CompilerQualifiedAliasSeparator`), so no reader can be admitted or refused by the tier anyway.
function scopedSpellingsOf(name ByteArray, claimants RangedTypeAliasArray, mints RangedTypeAliasArray) returns ByteArrayArray
	var spellings = ByteArrayArray.create()

	for claimant in claimants 'eachDistinctRange'
		let spelling = rangeQualifiedAliasName(name, alias: claimant)
		spellings.push(spelling)
		mints.push(mintedDeclaration(claimant, spelling: spelling, visibility: Visibility.public))
	end 'eachDistinctRange'

	return spellings
end 'scopedSpellingsOf'

// One MINTED declaration of a contested name — the same `underlying`, bounds and declaring file as the
// claimant it qualifies, under the qualified spelling.
function mintedDeclaration(claimant RangedTypeAlias, spelling ByteArray, visibility Visibility) returns RangedTypeAlias
	return RangedTypeAlias.create(spelling, underlying: claimant.underlying, low: claimant.low, high: claimant.high, visibility: visibility, declFilePath: claimant.declFilePath, nameLine: claimant.nameLine, nameColumn: claimant.nameColumn, domain: claimant.domain)
end 'mintedDeclaration'

// Has an earlier claimant already taken this RANGE? A LINEAR scan and not a set, because the list holds one
// entry per DISTINCT range of one name — one for nearly every name in any program, two or three where there
// is a contest at all — and a `Set` would cost an allocation per multiply-declared name to spare a
// comparison of two. It compares the BOUNDS and never a rendered name, which is what keeps the uncontested
// walk allocation-free (see `settleRangeContests`).
function holdsRange(claimants RangedTypeAliasArray, alias RangedTypeAlias) returns bool
	for held in claimants 'each'
		if held.hasSameRangeAs(alias) 'alreadyClaimed'
			return true
		end 'alreadyClaimed'
	end 'each'

	return false
end 'holdsRange'

function mintsOfDeclarations(name ByteArray, claimants RangedTypeAliasArray, mints RangedTypeAliasArray, provenance ClaimantProvenance) returns MintByDeclaringFile
	var spellings = MintByDeclaringFile.create()

	for claimant in claimants 'eachDeclaration'
		spellings.upsert(claimant.declFilePath.toString(), value: rangeQualifiedAliasName(name, alias: claimant))
	end 'eachDeclaration'

	let byFile = provenance.ordinalMints(spellings)

	for claimant in claimants 'eachMint'
		mints.push(mintedDeclaration(claimant, spelling: try byFile.get(claimant.declFilePath.toString()) otherwise panic("mintsOfDeclarations: '{claimant.declFilePath.toString()}' was minted in the pass above"), visibility: claimant.visibility))
	end 'eachMint'

	return byFile
end 'mintsOfDeclarations'

// ⭐ **DOES `low to high` CONTAIN EVERY BYTE VALUE, 0 THROUGH `u8.max`?**
// `holdsEveryByteInEveryFile`'s per-declaration test, spelled apart from it because it is a statement about
// a RANGE and not about the registry.
//
// ⚠ **IT HAS TWO READERS AND THEY ASK IT AT DIFFERENT QUANTIFIERS, WHICH IS WHY THE TEST ITSELF IS THE ONE
// EXPORTED THING.** `holdsEveryByteInEveryFile` above quantifies over every DECLARATION of a name,
// because its reader (`byteBufferBoundaryAdmits`) has only a name and no file. `Parser.requireRawByteFillFitsItsElement`
// holds a specific INSTANCE and therefore the reading file's own mint, so it asks about that one alias and
// nothing else. Two quantifiers over one predicate is the honest shape; a second spelling of `low == 0 and
// high >= u8.max` would be a second definition of what a byte is.
//
// ⚠⚠ **A NEGATIVE BOUND IS AMBIGUOUS AND IS ANSWERED NO — BOTH ENDS.** Bounds ride as the RAW 64-bit
// pattern (`RangedTypeAlias`'s header), so `-1` and `u64.max` are the SAME BITS: a negative `low` is either
// a genuine negative lower bound (which does contain 0) or a bound above `i64.max` (which does not), and
// nothing here can tell them apart. `rangedAliasStorageBytes` conflates them too and resolves it by taking
// the WIDEST slot, which is its safe direction; this door's safe direction is the opposite one — refuse —
// because its answer ADMITS a buffer of raw bytes into a declared element. So the lower bound must be
// EXACTLY zero, which is also the only lower bound a byte can honestly have, and a raw-negative `high`
// fails `>= u8.max` on the same bits for the same reason.
//
// ⚠ Nothing is lost by that: an element with a negative lower bound is not byte-packed either
// (`rangedAliasStorageBytes` gives it the machine word), so the boundary's stride test refuses it a second
// time. The two clauses agree here rather than one leaning on the other.
export function rangeHoldsEveryByte(low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt) returns bool
	return low == ByteValueSetLow and high >= ByteValueSetHigh
end 'rangeHoldsEveryByte'

// ⭐ **DOES THIS RANGE SPAN EVERY `int` — i.e. is the alias declaring it a pure second NAME for `int`?**
// EXACT on both ends, for the reason `spansEveryIntInEveryFile` states: the bounds are raw 64-bit patterns,
// so anything looser reads a bound above `i64.max` as one below zero. `I64MinBound`/`I64MaxBound` are the
// very constants `Parser.parseInt` enforces, so what "every int" means here cannot drift from what the lexer
// admits.
function rangeSpansEveryInt(low ParsedInt, high ParsedInt) returns bool
	return low == I64MinBound and high == I64MaxBound
end 'rangeSpansEveryInt'

// ⭐ **WHAT A BYTE'S VALUE SET IS, WRITTEN DOWN ONCE.** The test above measures a DECLARED element against
// these two bounds; `ProgramSignatures.elementIntegerValueRange` hands the same two out as the value set the
// COMPILER-OWNED byte element (`SynthesizedByteElementName`) admits, because a buffer the compiler minted is
// a run of raw bytes and has no declaration to read bounds off. Spelling `0` and `u8.max` at the second site
// would be a second definition of what a byte is — the thing this file's own header refuses at length.
export let ByteValueSetLow = 0 as ParsedInt
export let ByteValueSetHigh = u8.max as ParsedInt

// --- The TYPE-NAME namespace: one name, one declaration ---

// ⭐ **THE KIND OF DECLARATION THAT MINTED A TYPE NAME.** Five keywords mint one, and `typealias`
// mints THREE different things, so this is SEVEN cases and not five: the alias forms denote a
// primitive, a function type and a generic instance respectively, and only the first of them is
// resolved per declaring FILE. That difference is the whole axis the cross-file carve-out turns on
// (see `TypeNameRegistry`), so it may not be collapsed to the keyword.
//
// ⚠ **THE ALIAS FORMS ARE A CONTIGUOUS BAND, AND TWO RANGE ARMS READ IT** —
// `typeDeclKeyword` (they share one keyword) and `isTypeAliasDecl` (they share the carve-out and the
// code a collision earns). Append a new alias form INSIDE the band and any other kind BEFORE it; a
// kind inserted between `rangedAlias` and `genericAlias` is silently swallowed by both, with no
// missing-case error.
export enum TypeDeclKind
	structType
	enumType
	unionType
	interfaceType
	rangedAlias
	functionAlias
	genericAlias

	// ⭐ `typealias Pair = (Num, Num)` — a name for a synthesized TUPLE type. Appended at the END of
	// the alias band. Both arms below name it explicitly, as they name every other kind.
	tupleAlias
end 'TypeDeclKind'

// @generated library-cache begin
let CacheCasesTypeDeclKind = [TypeDeclKind.structType, TypeDeclKind.enumType, TypeDeclKind.unionType, TypeDeclKind.interfaceType, TypeDeclKind.rangedAlias, TypeDeclKind.functionAlias, TypeDeclKind.genericAlias, TypeDeclKind.tupleAlias]

module function cacheReadTypeDeclKind(reader LibraryCacheReader) returns TypeDeclKind throws LibraryCacheDamage
	let ordinal = try reader.unsigned()

	return try CacheCasesTypeDeclKind.get(ordinal as ElementIndex) otherwise throw LibraryCacheDamage.unknownTag(ordinal)
end 'cacheReadTypeDeclKind'

function cacheTableAgreesTypeDeclKind() returns bool
	let declared = TypeDeclKind.allCases

	if CacheCasesTypeDeclKind.count() != declared.count() 'differentCount'
		return false
	end 'differentCount'

	for position in 0 upto declared.count() 'eachCase'
		let listed = try CacheCasesTypeDeclKind.get(position) otherwise return false
		let expected = try declared.get(position) otherwise return false

		if listed != expected 'anotherCase'
			return false
		end 'anotherCase'
	end 'eachCase'

	return true
end 'cacheTableAgreesTypeDeclKind'

module function cacheEnumTablesAgreeInCompilerProject() returns bool
	if not cacheTableAgreesFunctionTypeDoor() 'disagreesCacheTableAgreesFunctionTypeDoor'
		return false
	end 'disagreesCacheTableAgreesFunctionTypeDoor'

	if not cacheTableAgreesDiscardDoor() 'disagreesCacheTableAgreesDiscardDoor'
		return false
	end 'disagreesCacheTableAgreesDiscardDoor'

	if not cacheTableAgreesVisibility() 'disagreesCacheTableAgreesVisibility'
		return false
	end 'disagreesCacheTableAgreesVisibility'

	if not cacheTableAgreesEnumBacking() 'disagreesCacheTableAgreesEnumBacking'
		return false
	end 'disagreesCacheTableAgreesEnumBacking'

	if not cacheTableAgreesEnumBackingLiteralKind() 'disagreesCacheTableAgreesEnumBackingLiteralKind'
		return false
	end 'disagreesCacheTableAgreesEnumBackingLiteralKind'

	if not cacheTableAgreesTypeDeclKind() 'disagreesCacheTableAgreesTypeDeclKind'
		return false
	end 'disagreesCacheTableAgreesTypeDeclKind'

	return true
end 'cacheEnumTablesAgreeInCompilerProject'

// @generated library-cache end

// The SOURCE KEYWORD that introduced a declaration of this kind — the word a diagnostic must call it
// by. The ONE place a `TypeDeclKind` is turned into that word (`EnumLayout.kindWord` reads it from
// here rather than spelling `enum`/`union` a second time). The three alias forms share `typealias`
// because that is what the author typed; the form distinction is a resolution fact, not a spelling
// one, and a message that invented `ranged typealias` would name a keyword no program contains.
//
// ⚠ **THESE FIVE LITERALS ARE A SECOND SPELLING OF `TokenKind`'s, AND THAT IS A MEASURED CHOICE, not
// an oversight.** `TokenKind.type.rawValue` and friends are where the language defines the words, and
// sourcing them from there COMPILES and costs **+26,503 code bytes** in the
// compiler (6,934,377 -> 6,960,880), because a string-backed enum's `rawValue` does not fold to its
// literal at a `gives` arm — it is a real accessor, not a constant. 26 KB is too much to spend on
// deleting five words. What holds the copies in step
// instead is the SUITE: `specs/type-name-collision.md` pins `type`, `typealias`, `interface` and
// `union` in `maxoncstderr` blocks, and `EnumLayout.kindWord` — its only other reader — is too,
// for `enum` and `union` across `specs/enum-match-exhaustive.md`. Rename a keyword: those fail.
export function typeDeclKeyword(kind TypeDeclKind) returns String
	return match kind 'k'
		structType gives "type"
		enumType gives "enum"
		unionType gives "union"
		interfaceType gives "interface"
		rangedAlias or
			functionAlias or
			genericAlias or
			tupleAlias gives "typealias"
	end 'k'
end 'typeDeclKeyword'

// Is this one of the `typealias` forms? It decides two things at once, which is why it is asked
// rather than re-derived: WHICH CODE a collision earns (E3061 is "two typealiases in scope declare
// the same name"; everything else is E3006) and whether a cross-file pair is legal at all.
export function isTypeAliasDecl(kind TypeDeclKind) returns bool
	return match kind 'k'
		structType or
			enumType or
			unionType or
			interfaceType gives false
		rangedAlias or
			functionAlias or
			genericAlias or
			tupleAlias gives true
	end 'k'
end 'isTypeAliasDecl'

// ⭐⭐ **WHO CLAIMED A TYPE NAME — the four facts, and the ONLY four, that decide whether two
// declarations of one name may COEXIST.** It is a record of its own rather than four fields on
// `TypeNameDecl` because the rule has TWO consumers holding two different records, and neither may own
// it: the MERGE-time duplicate check (`TypeNameRegistry`), which also needs the name and the position
// it blames, and the SWEEP-time contest table (`ProgramSignatures.crossKindContests`), which needs
// neither — it reports nothing, and it is keyed by the name. One record for the facts is what keeps
// `typeNamePairMayCoexist` a single function instead of one predicate per consumer.
//
// It carries NO POSITION on purpose. The sweep's declaration arrays do not all hold one
// (`StructLayout` and `EnumLayout` deliberately hold a `declFilePath` and nothing more — see
// `TypeNameDecl`), and inventing a `0:0` for them would be a sentinel wearing a real position's shape.
export type TypeNameClaim
	export let kind as TypeDeclKind
	export let declFilePath as FilePath

	// ⚠ **THE MODIFIER THE SOURCE WROTE, AND THE LIBRARY IS NOT EXEMPT FROM IT.** An unmarked `stdlib/`
	// alias claims its name at `file`, exactly as anyone's does; what makes the library's surface
	// nameable is the `public` its declarations carry.
	//
	// ⚠ It is NOT `UnusedExportCheck.declaredTypeVisibility`'s answer, which deliberately reports
	// `file` for the kinds that audit does not track. That one answers "is this worth auditing"; this
	// one answers "can another file see it".
	export let visibility as Visibility

	// ⭐ **WHICH LAYER DECLARED IT — `Parser.isStdlibSource`.**
	// A pair drawn from two layers can never meet, and that is the half of the rule a FORM test cannot
	// express: user code names a `stdlib/` declaration it never knew was there, and a `stdlib/` file
	// cannot see a user file at all.
	export let declaredByTheCorpus as bool

	export static function create(kind TypeDeclKind, declFilePath FilePath, visibility Visibility, declaredByTheCorpus bool) returns TypeNameClaim
		return Self{kind: kind, declFilePath: declFilePath, visibility: visibility, declaredByTheCorpus: declaredByTheCorpus}
	end 'create'

	// @generated library-cache begin
	module function cacheWrite(writer LibraryCacheWriter)
		writer.unsigned(self.kind.ordinal as CacheUnsigned)
		writer.text(self.declFilePath.toString())
		writer.unsigned(self.visibility.ordinal as CacheUnsigned)
		writer.flag(self.declaredByTheCorpus)
	end 'cacheWrite'

	module static function cacheRead(reader LibraryCacheReader) returns TypeNameClaim throws LibraryCacheDamage
		let f_kind = try cacheReadTypeDeclKind(reader)
		let f_declFilePath = try reader.path()
		let f_visibility = try cacheReadVisibility(reader)
		let f_declaredByTheCorpus = try reader.flag()

		return Self{kind: f_kind, declFilePath: f_declFilePath, visibility: f_visibility, declaredByTheCorpus: f_declaredByTheCorpus}
	end 'cacheRead'

	static function cacheLayoutBytes() returns CacheSize32
		return sizeof(TypeNameClaim) as CacheSize32
	end 'cacheLayoutBytes'

	// @generated library-cache end
end 'TypeNameClaim'

export typealias TypeNameClaimArray = Array with TypeNameClaim

// @generated library-cache begin
module function cacheWriteTypeNameClaimArray(writer LibraryCacheWriter, value TypeNameClaimArray)
	writer.unsigned(value.count() as CacheUnsigned)

	for item in value 'eachItem'
		item.cacheWrite(writer)
	end 'eachItem'
end 'cacheWriteTypeNameClaimArray'

module function cacheReadTypeNameClaimArray(reader LibraryCacheReader) returns TypeNameClaimArray throws LibraryCacheDamage
	let count = try reader.count()
	var result = TypeNameClaimArray.create()
	result.reserve(count)

	for _ in 0 upto count 'eachItem'
		let item = try TypeNameClaim.cacheRead(reader)
		result.push(item)
	end 'eachItem'

	return result
end 'cacheReadTypeNameClaimArray'

// @generated library-cache end

// ONE declaration that minted a type name, with where it was written. The position is the NAME
// token's, never the keyword's — a collision blames a declaration at its name, and
// `RangedTypeAlias` already anchors E3061 that way.
export type TypeNameDecl
	export let name as ByteArray

	// The four rule facts, in the record BOTH consumers of the rule read — see `TypeNameClaim`.
	export let claim as TypeNameClaim
	export let nameLine as LineNumber
	export let nameColumn as ColumnNumber

	export static function create(name ByteArray, claim TypeNameClaim, nameLine LineNumber, nameColumn ColumnNumber) returns TypeNameDecl
		return Self{name: name, claim: claim, nameLine: nameLine, nameColumn: nameColumn}
	end 'create'

	// @generated library-cache begin
	function cacheWrite(writer LibraryCacheWriter)
		writer.bytes(self.name)
		self.claim.cacheWrite(writer)
		writer.unsigned(self.nameLine as CacheUnsigned)
		writer.unsigned(self.nameColumn as CacheUnsigned)
	end 'cacheWrite'

	static function cacheRead(reader LibraryCacheReader) returns TypeNameDecl throws LibraryCacheDamage
		let f_name = try reader.bytes()
		let f_claim = try TypeNameClaim.cacheRead(reader)
		let f_nameLine = (try reader.unsigned()) as LineNumber
		let f_nameColumn = (try reader.unsigned()) as ColumnNumber

		return Self{name: f_name, claim: f_claim, nameLine: f_nameLine, nameColumn: f_nameColumn}
	end 'cacheRead'

	static function cacheLayoutBytes() returns CacheSize32
		return sizeof(TypeNameDecl) as CacheSize32
	end 'cacheLayoutBytes'

	// @generated library-cache end
end 'TypeNameDecl'

// One file's type-name declarations, IN SOURCE ORDER. The order is the point and it is why this is
// one array rather than a position added to each of the four layout types: `structTypes`,
// `enumTypes`, `interfaces` and `typeAliases` are separate arrays, so which of a file's `type Box`
// and `typealias Box` came SECOND is a fact none of them holds. Blaming the wrong one of a pair is
// exactly the misdiagnosis this rule exists to remove.
export typealias TypeNameDeclArray = Array with TypeNameDecl

// @generated library-cache begin
module function cacheWriteTypeNameDeclArray(writer LibraryCacheWriter, value TypeNameDeclArray)
	writer.unsigned(value.count() as CacheUnsigned)

	for item in value 'eachItem'
		item.cacheWrite(writer)
	end 'eachItem'
end 'cacheWriteTypeNameDeclArray'

module function cacheReadTypeNameDeclArray(reader LibraryCacheReader) returns TypeNameDeclArray throws LibraryCacheDamage
	let count = try reader.count()
	var result = TypeNameDeclArray.create()
	result.reserve(count)

	for _ in 0 upto count 'eachItem'
		let item = try TypeNameDecl.cacheRead(reader)
		result.push(item)
	end 'eachItem'

	return result
end 'cacheReadTypeNameDeclArray'

// @generated library-cache end

export type SignatureTypeDemand
	export let demandingFunction as ByteArray
	export let demandingTier as Visibility
	export let typeName as ByteArray
	export let declFilePath as FilePath

	export static function create(demandingFunction ByteArray, demandingTier Visibility, typeName ByteArray, declFilePath FilePath) returns SignatureTypeDemand
		return Self{demandingFunction: demandingFunction, demandingTier: demandingTier, typeName: typeName, declFilePath: declFilePath}
	end 'create'
end 'SignatureTypeDemand'

export typealias SignatureTypeDemandArray = Array with SignatureTypeDemand

type AdmittedTypeNameClaim
	export let claim as TypeNameClaim
	export let id as ElementIndex

	static function create(claim TypeNameClaim, id ElementIndex) returns AdmittedTypeNameClaim
		return Self{claim: claim, id: id}
	end 'create'
end 'AdmittedTypeNameClaim'

typealias AdmittedTypeNameClaimMap = Map with (ByteArray, AdmittedTypeNameClaim)

typealias CoexistingTypeNameClaimMap = Map with (ByteArray, TypeNameRefuserSlots)

typealias AdmittedClaimIdArray = Array with ElementIndex

// The claims of one name that can refuse a newcomer from another file, kept only where no held
// claim already refuses everything the new one would (`claimTakesARefusalSlot`), so a newcomer is
// tested against a few slots rather than against every declaration of the name.
type TypeNameRefuserSlots
	export var ids as AdmittedClaimIdArray
	export var claims as TypeNameClaimArray

	static function create() returns TypeNameRefuserSlots
		return Self{ids: AdmittedClaimIdArray.create(), claims: TypeNameClaimArray.create()}
	end 'create'

	function note(admitted AdmittedTypeNameClaim, directories ModuleDirectoryByFile)
		if claimTakesARefusalSlot(admitted.claim, refusers: self.claims, directories: directories) 'refusesWhatNoEarlierDeclarationDoes'
			self.ids.push(admitted.id)
			self.claims.push(admitted.claim)
		end 'refusesWhatNoEarlierDeclarationDoes'
	end 'note'

	function idAt(slot ElementIndex) returns ElementIndex
		return try self.ids.get(slot) otherwise panic("TypeNameRefuserSlots.idAt: slot {slot} is past the {self.ids.count()} held — `firstRefusalAmong` answers a slot of the parallel claim column")
	end 'idAt'

	function nominalId() returns ElementIndex throws MapError
		for (iter, claim) in self.claims.withIterator() 'eachClaim'
			if not isTypeAliasDecl(claim.kind) 'theNominalOne'
				return self.idAt(iter.index())
			end 'theNominalOne'
		end 'eachClaim'

		throw MapError.keyNotFound
	end 'nominalId'
end 'TypeNameRefuserSlots'

union TypeNameAdmissionVerdict
	admitted
	refusedBy(id ElementIndex)
end 'TypeNameAdmissionVerdict'

// ⭐⭐ **MAY ONE MORE DECLARATION OF A TYPE NAME JOIN THE ONES ALREADY ADMITTED?** The one
// implementation behind `TypeNameRegistry.record`, which reports a refusal, and
// `everyClaimMayCoexist`, which decides the names the cross-kind contest must tell apart — so the
// compiler cannot refuse a pair it also resolves, or resolve one it refuses. Three indexes, three
// refusals:
//
//   • `nameableInDirectory` — two declarations nameable outside their files, from two FILES of one
//     module directory: both are spelled `dir.Name`, so no qualification tells them apart;
//   • `crossFile` — the pair rule, `typeNamePairMayCoexist`, against the name's refusal slots;
//   • `sameFile` — one file declaring a name twice, whatever the kinds.
type TypeNameAdmission
	var crossFile as CoexistingTypeNameClaimMap
	var sameFile as AdmittedTypeNameClaimMap
	var nameableInDirectory as AdmittedTypeNameClaimMap

	static function create() returns TypeNameAdmission
		return Self{crossFile: CoexistingTypeNameClaimMap.create(), sameFile: AdmittedTypeNameClaimMap.create(), nameableInDirectory: AdmittedTypeNameClaimMap.create()}
	end 'create'

	function admit(name ByteArray, claim TypeNameClaim, id ElementIndex, directories ModuleDirectoryByFile) returns TypeNameAdmissionVerdict
		if not nameableOutsideItsFile(claim) 'reachableFromItsOwnFileAlone'
			return self.admitAcrossDirectories(name, claim: claim, id: id, directories: directories)
		end 'reachableFromItsOwnFileAlone'

		let directoryKey = directoryScopedTypeNameKey(name, declFilePath: claim.declFilePath, directories: directories)

		if let incumbent = try self.nameableInDirectory.get(directoryKey) 'thisDirectoryAlreadyNamesIt'
			if not incumbent.claim.declFilePath.equals(claim.declFilePath) 'anotherFileOfThisDirectory'
				return TypeNameAdmissionVerdict.refusedBy(incumbent.id)
			end 'anotherFileOfThisDirectory'
		end 'thisDirectoryAlreadyNamesIt'

		let verdict = self.admitAcrossDirectories(name, claim: claim, id: id, directories: directories)

		match verdict 'verdict'
			admitted then self.nameableInDirectory.upsert(directoryKey, value: AdmittedTypeNameClaim.create(claim, id: id))
			refusedBy then break 'verdict'
		end 'verdict'

		return verdict
	end 'admit'

	function admitAcrossDirectories(name ByteArray, claim TypeNameClaim, id ElementIndex, directories ModuleDirectoryByFile) returns TypeNameAdmissionVerdict
		var refusers = try self.crossFile.get(name) otherwise TypeNameRefuserSlots.create()

		match firstRefusalAmong(refusers.claims, newcomer: claim, directories: directories) 'slots'
			admitted then break 'slots'
			refusedBy(slot) then return TypeNameAdmissionVerdict.refusedBy(refusers.idAt(slot))
		end 'slots'

		let fileKey = fileScopedDeclKey(name, declFilePath: claim.declFilePath)

		if let incumbent = try self.sameFile.get(fileKey) 'thisFileAlreadyDeclaresIt'
			return TypeNameAdmissionVerdict.refusedBy(incumbent.id)
		end 'thisFileAlreadyDeclaresIt'

		let admitted = AdmittedTypeNameClaim.create(claim, id: id)
		refusers.note(admitted, directories: directories)
		self.crossFile.upsert(name, value: refusers)
		self.sameFile.upsert(fileKey, value: admitted)
		return TypeNameAdmissionVerdict.admitted
	end 'admitAcrossDirectories'

	function nominalIdOf(name ByteArray) returns ElementIndex throws MapError
		let refusers = try self.crossFile.get(name)
		return try refusers.nominalId()
	end 'nominalIdOf'
end 'TypeNameAdmission'

union TypeNameRefusal
	admitted
	refusedBy(slot ElementIndex)
end 'TypeNameRefusal'

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT ONE DECLARATION CAN REFUSE FROM A DIFFERENT FILE, AS A VALUE** — its kind, its tier and
// its layer, which is all `typeNamePairMayCoexist` reads beyond the module directory
// `TypeNameAdmission` checks on its own. `TypeNameRefuserSlots` keeps a claim only where no held
// claim of its slot already refuses everything it would; two library aliases are the one slot where
// that turns on what each one's reach contains (`heldRefusesEverythingTheNewcomerWould`).
//
// ⚠ **A FILE-PRIVATE ALIAS IS `none`, AND THAT IS THE RULE RATHER THAN A GAP.** It is reachable from the
// one file that declared it, so the only pair it can be half of is a SAME-FILE one, which
// `TypeNameAdmission.sameFile` answers.
//
// ⚠ The NOMINAL half is split by layer even though at most one nominal declaration of a name is
// ever ACCEPTED: `everyClaimMayCoexist` is handed claims that have not been through the merge, and
// the layer decides which aliases a nominal meets.
enum TypeNameRefusalSlot
	none
	corpusNominal
	userNominal
	corpusAlias
	userAlias
end 'TypeNameRefusalSlot'

function typeNameRefusalSlot(claim TypeNameClaim) returns TypeNameRefusalSlot
	if not isTypeAliasDecl(claim.kind) 'aNominalDeclaration'
		return TypeNameRefusalSlot.corpusNominal if claim.declaredByTheCorpus else TypeNameRefusalSlot.userNominal
	end 'aNominalDeclaration'

	if claim.visibility == Visibility.file 'reachableFromItsOwnFileAlone'
		return TypeNameRefusalSlot.none
	end 'reachableFromItsOwnFileAlone'

	return TypeNameRefusalSlot.corpusAlias if claim.declaredByTheCorpus else TypeNameRefusalSlot.userAlias
end 'typeNameRefusalSlot'

// What ONE declaration collided with, and therefore which diagnostic it earns. `accepted` covers
// both "first declaration of this name" and the legal cross-file alias case; the collision arm
// carries the INCUMBENT, because the reporter blames the newcomer and names the incumbent's keyword.
export union TypeNameRecordOutcome
	accepted
	collides(winner TypeNameDecl)
end 'TypeNameRecordOutcome'

export type TypeNameCollision
	export let decl as TypeNameDecl
	export let winner as TypeNameDecl

	export static function create(decl TypeNameDecl, winner TypeNameDecl) returns TypeNameCollision
		return Self{decl: decl, winner: winner}
	end 'create'

	// @generated library-cache begin
	function cacheWrite(writer LibraryCacheWriter)
		self.decl.cacheWrite(writer)
		self.winner.cacheWrite(writer)
	end 'cacheWrite'

	static function cacheRead(reader LibraryCacheReader) returns TypeNameCollision throws LibraryCacheDamage
		let f_decl = try TypeNameDecl.cacheRead(reader)
		let f_winner = try TypeNameDecl.cacheRead(reader)

		return Self{decl: f_decl, winner: f_winner}
	end 'cacheRead'

	static function cacheLayoutBytes() returns CacheSize16
		return sizeof(TypeNameCollision) as CacheSize16
	end 'cacheLayoutBytes'

	// @generated library-cache end
end 'TypeNameCollision'

export typealias TypeNameCollisionArray = Array with TypeNameCollision

// @generated library-cache begin
module function cacheWriteTypeNameCollisionArray(writer LibraryCacheWriter, value TypeNameCollisionArray)
	writer.unsigned(value.count() as CacheUnsigned)

	for item in value 'eachItem'
		item.cacheWrite(writer)
	end 'eachItem'
end 'cacheWriteTypeNameCollisionArray'

module function cacheReadTypeNameCollisionArray(reader LibraryCacheReader) returns TypeNameCollisionArray throws LibraryCacheDamage
	let count = try reader.count()
	var result = TypeNameCollisionArray.create()
	result.reserve(count)

	for _ in 0 upto count 'eachItem'
		let item = try TypeNameCollision.cacheRead(reader)
		result.push(item)
	end 'eachItem'

	return result
end 'cacheReadTypeNameCollisionArray'

// @generated library-cache end

// ⭐ **THE TYPE-NAME REGISTRY — the ONE decider of "is this type name already declared?"** (E3006,
// E3061). Without it, which of a `type Box` and a `typealias Box` a reference meant would be
// decided by `denotedNamedType`'s arm order and the merge order, never by the author.
//
// **THE RULE: a type name is declared exactly once IN ANY ONE SCOPE.** Two declarations that cannot
// SEE each other are not two claims on one name; they are two names that happen to be spelled
// alike. `TypeNameAdmission` applies it, so the cross-kind contest
// (`ProgramSignatures.settleCrossKindContests`, through `everyClaimMayCoexist`), which decides
// which of two coexisting declarations a file MEANS, reads the identical rule.
//
// ⚠ **THIS REGISTRY HOLDS ONLY THE NAMES A DECLARATION SPELLS, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE NAMESPACE.** A
// generic instantiation spells no name: it COMPILES to a string every per-type symbol is derived
// from (`Box_String` ⇒ `__destruct_Box_String`), and when that string is one a declaration already
// holds, `ProgramSignatures.mangleGenericInstance` mints it behind the reserved `__` prefix
// instead. The compiled half is therefore DISJOINT from this one by construction, so nothing here
// is contestable and this registry has no instantiation reader at all — which is why it answers
// only `record` and `recordAll`. `ParseStaging.checkTypeSymbolNamespace` owns the compiled half, as
// a check between two INSTANTIATIONS that never consults a declaration.
export type TypeNameRegistry
	var admission as TypeNameAdmission
	var accepted as TypeNameDeclArray

	export static function create() returns TypeNameRegistry
		return Self{admission: TypeNameAdmission.create(), accepted: TypeNameDeclArray.create()}
	end 'create'

	export function recordAll(decls TypeNameDeclArray, directories ModuleDirectoryByFile) returns TypeNameCollisionArray
		var collisions = TypeNameCollisionArray.create()

		for decl in decls 'eachDecl'
			match self.record(decl, directories: directories) 'collision'
				accepted then continue
				collides(winner) then collisions.push(TypeNameCollision.create(decl, winner: winner))
			end 'collision'
		end 'eachDecl'

		return collisions
	end 'recordAll'

	export function record(decl TypeNameDecl, directories ModuleDirectoryByFile) returns TypeNameRecordOutcome
		match self.admission.admit(decl.name, claim: decl.claim, id: self.accepted.count(), directories: directories) 'admission'
			admitted then break 'admission'
			refusedBy(id) then return TypeNameRecordOutcome.collides(self.acceptedAt(id))
		end 'admission'

		self.accepted.push(decl)
		return TypeNameRecordOutcome.accepted
	end 'record'

	export function declarationOf(name ByteArray) returns TypeNameDecl throws MapError
		return self.acceptedAt(try self.admission.nominalIdOf(name))
	end 'declarationOf'

	function acceptedAt(id ElementIndex) returns TypeNameDecl
		return try self.accepted.get(id) otherwise panic("TypeNameRegistry.acceptedAt: id {id} is past the {self.accepted.count()} accepted declarations — an admission id is the position its declaration was accepted at")
	end 'acceptedAt'
end 'TypeNameRegistry'

function nameableOutsideItsFile(claim TypeNameClaim) returns bool
	return not isTypeAliasDecl(claim.kind) or claim.visibility != Visibility.file
end 'nameableOutsideItsFile'

function directoryScopedTypeNameKey(name ByteArray, declFilePath FilePath, directories ModuleDirectoryByFile) returns ByteArray
	return "{moduleDirectoryIn(directories, path: declFilePath.toString())}{DirectoryScopedKeySeparator}{String.from(name)}".toByteArray()
end 'directoryScopedTypeNameKey'

let DirectoryScopedKeySeparator = "|"

module function everyClaimMayCoexist(name ByteArray, claims TypeNameClaimArray, directories ModuleDirectoryByFile) returns bool
	var admission = TypeNameAdmission.create()

	for (iter, claim) in claims.withIterator() 'eachClaim'
		match admission.admit(name, claim: claim, id: iter.index(), directories: directories) 'admission'
			admitted then continue
			refusedBy then return false
		end 'admission'
	end 'eachClaim'

	return true
end 'everyClaimMayCoexist'

function claimTakesARefusalSlot(claim TypeNameClaim, refusers TypeNameClaimArray, directories ModuleDirectoryByFile) returns bool
	if typeNameRefusalSlot(claim) == TypeNameRefusalSlot.none 'itCanRefuseOnlyItsOwnFile'
		return false
	end 'itCanRefuseOnlyItsOwnFile'

	for held in refusers 'eachHeldSlot'
		if heldRefusesEverythingTheNewcomerWould(held, newcomer: claim, directories: directories) 'anEarlierClaimRefusesEverythingThisOneWould'
			return false
		end 'anEarlierClaimRefusesEverythingThisOneWould'
	end 'eachHeldSlot'

	return true
end 'claimTakesARefusalSlot'

function firstRefusalAmong(refusers TypeNameClaimArray, newcomer TypeNameClaim, directories ModuleDirectoryByFile) returns TypeNameRefusal
	for slot in 0 upto refusers.count() 'eachRefusingSlot'
		let refuser = try refusers.get(slot) otherwise panic("firstRefusalAmong: refusers.get OOB at {slot} below count {refusers.count()}")

		if not typeNamePairMayCoexist(refuser, newcomer: newcomer, directories: directories) 'theTwoMeet'
			return TypeNameRefusal.refusedBy(slot)
		end 'theTwoMeet'
	end 'eachRefusingSlot'

	return TypeNameRefusal.admitted
end 'firstRefusalAmong'

function heldRefusesEverythingTheNewcomerWould(held TypeNameClaim, newcomer TypeNameClaim, directories ModuleDirectoryByFile) returns bool
	let slot = typeNameRefusalSlot(newcomer)

	if typeNameRefusalSlot(held) != slot 'anotherSlot'
		return false
	end 'anotherSlot'

	return match slot 'slot'
		corpusAlias gives libraryAliasReachContains(held, inner: newcomer, directories: directories)
		none or
			corpusNominal or
			userNominal or
			userAlias gives true
	end 'slot'
end 'heldRefusesEverythingTheNewcomerWould'

// ⭐⭐ **MAY TWO DECLARATIONS OF ONE TYPE NAME, FROM TWO FILES, COEXIST?** — the pair rule
// `TypeNameAdmission` applies against the name's refusal slots. Refused:
//
//   • **ONE FILE, TWICE** — whatever the kinds. No qualification could ever disambiguate the pair,
//     and it is tested FIRST so no later arm can admit it.
//   • **TWO NOMINAL DECLARATIONS.** A `type`/`enum`/`union`/`interface` name is whole-program.
//   • **TWO LIBRARY ALIASES SOME READER CAN NAME BOTH OF** (`libraryAliasesSomeReaderNamesBoth`) —
//     E3061.
//   • **A NOMINAL AND A NAMEABLE ALIAS OF ONE LAYER IN ONE MODULE DIRECTORY**, which share
//     `dir.Name`.
//
// Every other pair coexists: two aliases a reader may both name are an ambiguous REFERENCE, E3063
// at the use; a FILE-PRIVATE alias is reachable from its own file alone; a `stdlib/` declaration
// and a user one are told apart by layer (a user declaration against a library NOMINAL never
// arrives here at all — `ProgramSignatures.contestStdlibTypeName` has already moved the library's
// declaration to `__Name`); and a nominal and an alias in two module directories are each reachable
// as `dir.Name`.
function typeNamePairMayCoexist(winner TypeNameClaim, newcomer TypeNameClaim, directories ModuleDirectoryByFile) returns bool
	if winner.declFilePath.equals(newcomer.declFilePath) 'oneFileDeclaredItTwice'
		return false
	end 'oneFileDeclaredItTwice'

	let winnerIsAlias = isTypeAliasDecl(winner.kind)
	let newcomerIsAlias = isTypeAliasDecl(newcomer.kind)

	if winnerIsAlias and newcomerIsAlias 'twoAliases'
		return not libraryAliasesSomeReaderNamesBoth(winner, newcomer: newcomer, directories: directories)
	end 'twoAliases'

	if not winnerIsAlias and not newcomerIsAlias 'twoNominalDeclarations'
		return false
	end 'twoNominalDeclarations'

	let alias = winner if winnerIsAlias else newcomer

	if alias.visibility == Visibility.file 'theAliasIsReachableFromItsOwnFileAlone'
		return true
	end 'theAliasIsReachableFromItsOwnFileAlone'

	if winner.declaredByTheCorpus != newcomer.declaredByTheCorpus 'twoLayers'
		return true
	end 'twoLayers'

	return moduleDirectoryIn(directories, path: winner.declFilePath.toString()) != moduleDirectoryIn(directories, path: newcomer.declFilePath.toString())
end 'typeNamePairMayCoexist'

function libraryAliasesSomeReaderNamesBoth(winner TypeNameClaim, newcomer TypeNameClaim, directories ModuleDirectoryByFile) returns bool
	if typeNameRefusalSlot(winner) != TypeNameRefusalSlot.corpusAlias or typeNameRefusalSlot(newcomer) != TypeNameRefusalSlot.corpusAlias 'notTwoNameableLibraryAliases'
		return false
	end 'notTwoNameableLibraryAliases'

	return libraryAliasReachContains(winner, inner: newcomer, directories: directories) or libraryAliasReachContains(newcomer, inner: winner, directories: directories)
end 'libraryAliasesSomeReaderNamesBoth'

function libraryAliasReachContains(outer TypeNameClaim, inner TypeNameClaim, directories ModuleDirectoryByFile) returns bool
	return match inner.visibility 'innerTier'
		global or
			public gives claimReachesEveryReader(outer, directories: directories)
		file or
			module gives visibleThroughModuleDirectories(directories, visibility: outer.visibility, declPath: outer.declFilePath.toString(), readerPath: inner.declFilePath.toString())
	end 'innerTier'
end 'libraryAliasReachContains'

function claimReachesEveryReader(claim TypeNameClaim, directories ModuleDirectoryByFile) returns bool
	return match claim.visibility 'tier'
		global or
			public gives true
		file gives false
		module gives moduleDirectoryIn(directories, path: claim.declFilePath.toString()).isEmpty()
	end 'tier'
end 'claimReachesEveryReader'

// --- Range-check sites — the parser's carrier for `InsertRangeChecks` ---
//
// ⭐ WHY A SIDE TABLE AND NOT A MARKER OP. A value meeting a ranged alias produces nothing of its
// own — a narrowing `as` cast RETAGS its operand and flows it on unchanged, and a store writes the
// value the source already named — so there is nothing for an IR op to define, and every position
// that must be checked (an `as`-cast operand, a ranged `return`, a struct-literal field, a field
// store, a field's declared default, an array element, a call argument) is found by the parser at
// PARSE time, not by any later structural walk. A dedicated `MaxonOp` would ALSO force a new arm
// into every exhaustive `MaxonOp` match — including the escape-analysis functions
// — so the site is recorded HERE instead, keyed by the enclosing
// function's (unique) name, and resolved by the Std-tier `InsertRangeChecks` pass.
//
// ⭐ **THE SIDE TABLE IS ALSO WHAT MAKES THE ERASURE SURVIVABLE, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN.**
// `TypeResolution.resolveNamedType` collapses a plain ranged alias to its primitive and DROPS the
// range, and `syncResolvedSignature` writes that back into `project.funcSignatures` — so by
// `semanticCheck` a parameter's declared `Percent` is bare `int`. Recording the NAME here, off the
// still-named `MaxonType`, is immune to that by construction.
//
// The bounds live only in `typeAliases` (an alias resolves AWAY to its primitive before the
// pass runs), so a site carries the alias NAME and the pass looks the bounds up — the linchpin
// the "retain the alias" requirement exists for. `value` is the flat Std `ValueId` the operand
// carries (ids pass through `lowerMaxonToStd` verbatim), never a name string. `line`/`column`
// anchor the E3005 diagnostic and the runtime panic message on the source position.

// ⭐ **WHICH HALVES OF THE RULE A POSITION OWES.** `range-check-panic.md` states both — a compile-time
// E3005 for a value the compiler can fold, and a runtime check where an unfoldable value LANDS — and
// **EVERY POSITION NOW OWES BOTH EXCEPT ONE: the CALL ARGUMENT AT A CONCRETE PARAMETER, which owes the
// compile-time half alone.**
//
// The reason that one position can owe only the first half is mechanical: a runtime check is a BRANCH, so
// it splits the block it lands in, and a call argument is evaluated part-way through building an argument
// list. Guards are emitted a tier later, so a block is never split mid-argument-list — and there is
// no alias to check against at the argument either: the parameter belongs to a callee that may be
// declared in another file (`RangeCheckArgSite`), so no positioned site can be recorded there.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AN ARGUMENT AT AN *OPAQUE* PARAMETER IS THE EXCEPTION'S OWN EXCEPTION, AND IT OWES BOTH
// HALVES.** The exemption above is not really "a call argument cannot be guarded" — it is "the
// CALLEE'S ENTRY guards it instead". A parameter declared `value T` has no range at that entry,
// because a generic body is compiled ONCE for every instantiation, so the exemption has nothing to
// hand the obligation to. The CALL SITE knows the substitution and therefore owes the whole rule;
// it records an ordinary alias-named site (below) at the position the `call` op is about to take,
// which puts the cascade exactly where the entry guard would have stood. See
// `Parser.recordOpaqueArgRangeChecks`.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE OBSTACLE MOVES THE GUARD, IT DOES NOT REMOVE IT.** The runtime half of the argument door
// belongs to the CALLEE'S ENTRY instead: one guard per narrowed parameter per function, standing in front
// of every caller including the indirect ones (`RangeCheckParamSite`,
// `InsertRangeChecks.guardRangedParams`). So an argument site owes only the compile-time half, and the
// premise is enforced all the same.
//
// ⭐ **THE ARRAY ELEMENT OWES BOTH HALVES TOO, AND IS GUARDED FROM THE OTHER SIDE.** The entry guard
// cannot reach it — the element travels into a shared `Array` body whose
// parameter is the OPAQUE element type, so there is no narrowed parameter for an entry guard to stand
// behind. But the obstacle above never applied to it either: an element's alias is known at PARSE time,
// in the instance's declared element type, so it records an ordinary positioned site and its guard goes
// at the STORE, in front of the `__managed_*` call. See `Parser.requireContainerElementType`.
//
// ⚠ **WHAT IS STILL UNENFORCED IS NOT A POSITION** — it is the zero-initialized slot `Array.resize`
// EXPOSES, which no value ever crosses into. Pinned by `specs/safety.md`'s
// `divide-by-zero-fault-through-a-resized-array-slot`.
// ⚠ `module`, not `export`: `InsertRangeChecks` is the only reader and it sits under `Compiler/`, and
// `checkUnusedExports` (E3093) is what says so — the SELF-COMPILE runs that pass.
module enum RangeCheckGuard
	runtime
	compileTimeOnly
end 'RangeCheckGuard'

// One place a value MEETS a ranged alias whose name the parser already holds — an `as` cast, a
// struct-literal field, a field store, a field's declared default, an array element. `block` is the
// function-local BlockId the value is checked in (the parser's on-the-fly-SSA current block) — the guard
// must split THAT block, not the value's DEFINITION block. An int->int cast is a representational no-op
// with no Std op to anchor to, so without the block a cast of a value defined earlier (a parameter, or a
// value from a dominating block) would place its guard at the definition — which runs UNCONDITIONALLY,
// even when the cast sits in an unselected ternary arm / if-branch. BlockIds survive `lowerMaxonToStd`
// (blocks are cloned 1:1 keeping their id), so the Std pass finds the same block by id.
//
// ⚠ **THEY SURVIVE THE LOWERING; THEY DO NOT SURVIVE THE PARSER, AND ONE CONSTRUCT REWRITES THEM.** A
// ternary's TRUE ARM is parsed onto the unconditional path and then RELOCATED into a block minted
// afterwards, so a site recorded inside it names a block its value has left. `Parser.rehomeArmRangeSites`
// moves this pair with the ops — that function's header carries the two wrong answers it costs, one of
// which stopped the compiler compiling itself.
//
// ⭐⭐ **`maxonOpPos` IS WHERE IN THAT BLOCK, AND IT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN A PATH ANCHOR AND A
// STATEMENT ANCHOR.** The block alone says which path the guard runs on; without a position the guard
// can only go at the END of that block, so the whole rest of the block — every print, every call, every
// store and every division the out-of-range value reaches — runs BEFORE the check that forbids it:
// `b.v = bad` then `print("{b.v}")` prints 500 back out of a `Percent` field, and `z as NonZero`
// with z == 0 followed by `100 / d` dies `integer divide by zero` instead of raising the range
// panic the language promises. Both exit 1, which is why no exit-code-only case can see either.
//
// It is the count of ops the recording block held at the moment the value was checked — i.e. the
// ordinal the CONSUMING op is about to take — because a cast is recorded BEFORE the store/call/division
// that spends its value is emitted. An int->int cast emits no op at all, so a MAXON ordinal is the only
// anchor there is.
//
// ⭐ **TWO POSITIONS, TWO TIERS, AND THE SITE CARRIES BOTH.** `maxonOpPos` is the PARSER's answer and
// means nothing at the Std tier, where each Maxon op has become N; `stdOpPos` is `LowerMaxonToStd`'s
// translation of it (`RangeSiteWalk`), and it is what `InsertRangeChecks` splits at. They are separate
// fields rather than one rewritten in place because a field that changes meaning at a tier boundary is
// a field the next reader has to date. `stdOpPos` is 0 until the lowering fills it, which
// `FunctionRangeChecks.positionsResolved` is the presence bit for — a `0` is a legitimate position (a
// guard at the very top of its block), so no value of it can mean "unset".
// ⚠ **IT CARRIES NO `RangeCheckGuard`, AND CANNOT.** Every position that records one of these owes
// BOTH halves — the array element's guard goes at the STORE (`Parser.requireContainerElementType`).
// A field holding one value at every site is a fact written twice, and the copy that goes stale is
// the prose. The constant call-argument door (`RangeCheckArgSite`) is the one position that still
// owes the compile-time half alone, and it holds none of these: it asks `guardVerdict` directly
// (`InsertRangeChecks.checkArgAgainstParamRange`).
// ⚠ **AN ARGUMENT AT AN OPAQUE `T` PARAMETER RECORDS ONE OF THESE**, and is not a counter-example
// to the paragraph above: its alias is not the CALLEE's to name — the callee has none — it is the
// receiver instance's substituted type argument, which the call site holds. So it is an ordinary
// alias-named door like every other, and it owes both halves like every other.
