	// declared with an alias two files declare differently carries a name that cannot be resolved
	// until every file has folded. That resolution happens once, at
	// `ProgramSignatures.resolveRecordedGenericAliasTypes`, and lands here.
	//
	// ⚠ It rewrites ONE DECLARED column, which is why it is not a violation of `addField`'s "one
	// writer" rule: the columns stay in lockstep because no length changes. It is deliberately not
	// general — there is exactly one caller per REASON, and a further reason to retype a recorded
	// field would be a further reason for two passes to disagree about what a declaration says. The
	// reasons are the three facts the sweep structurally cannot answer, each settled once with every
	// file folded: the contested generic ALIAS (above), the swept `named` that denotes an INTERFACE
	// (`normalizeSweptInterfaceTypes`), and the swept `named` that is a BARE GENERIC NAME at the
	// declaring type's own parameters (`normalizeSweptScopedGenericFields`).
