// ⇒ A seed stands in for a DECLARATION and owes every clause that declaration writes, not just its cases.
// ⚠ `module`: `ServiceCompanions.buildServiceReplyErrorEnum` synthesizes an error enum too,
// and it owes `implements Error` for the reason this header gives — a `throws Calc.divide.errors` clause is
// checked by `ConformanceCheck.requireImplErrorConformsAndIsScalar` like any other.
module function builtinErrorConformance() returns ByteArrayArray
	var conformsTo = ByteArrayArray.create()
	conformsTo.push(ErrorInterfaceName)
	return conformsTo
end 'builtinErrorConformance'

// EnumLayout — one declared `enum`/`union`: what it is called, whether it is a `union` (the ONLY
// thing that separates the two — identical lowering, different diagnostic wording), and its cases
// IN DECLARATION ORDER.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE PARALLEL-COLUMN SHAPE AVOIDS THE "ONE FIELD WITH TWO MEANINGS" HAZARD.** A single
// ordinal column would be both the auto-increment TAG counter an explicit `= N` resets and the
// case's DECLARATION POSITION, and reading one as the other is a silent wrong answer (`ok = 500
// notFound = 200` matching nothing). Here
// the case's DECLARATION INDEX is the array position (`indexOfCase`, derived, never stored) and the
// case's TAG is the `caseTags[i]` column. The two are SEPARATE BY CONSTRUCTION: no field can carry
// both, so an arm reading the index against a tag-valued scrutinee — the exact bug — cannot be
// written. It is the enum twin of `StructLayout`'s field columns, and `addCase` is the SOLE writer
// for `StructLayout.addField`'s reason: a caller that pushed a name and forgot a tag would shift
// every later case's tag onto the wrong case.
//
// **TAGS ARE UNIFORMLY `ParsedInt` (i64), for int AND float backing.** An int-backed case's tag is
// its raw value; a FLOAT-backed case's tag is its IEEE-754 BIT PATTERN — the same representation
// `FieldDefault.literal` stores a float default as. An enum value is therefore always an i64 (the
// tag, or the float bits), living in a GENERAL-PURPOSE register and matched by an i64 compare, so a
// `match` needs no float register class and no second dispatch path.
//
// ⚠⚠ **BUT A SITE THAT CONSUMES THE VALUE AS A *NUMBER* MUST DECODE IT, AND THE TAG COLUMN CANNOT TELL
// IT TO.** The uniform i64 is a representation decision, not a claim that a float-backed enum IS an
// integer: `"{w}"` printed `4612811918334230528` for `light = 2.5`, `Weight.light * scale` multiplied bit
// patterns, and `below < mid` ordered two NEGATIVE cases backwards, all silently. What decides is
// `backing` below, read at each such site and paid for with a `bitsToFloat` REINTERPRETATION — never a
// `cvtsi2sd`, which would convert the encoding as if it were a magnitude.
// See `Parser.floatBehindEnumValue` for the one door every one of those sites goes through.

// ⭐ **THE ENUM ACCESSOR the compiler READS — `s.ordinal`, a case's DECLARATION INDEX.** Spelled beside the
// layout because the layout is what answers it (`tagsAreDeclarationIndices`), and named rather than
// inlined at `Parser.enumAccessorFieldAt` so the member's spelling and the rule that decides it cannot
// come apart.
//
// ⚠ **ITS TWO INSTANCE SIBLINGS ARE `EnumNameMemberName` AND `EnumRawValueMemberName` BELOW, AND ALL THREE
// ARE NOW ONE MECHANISM.** The note that stood here called a name "a value that has to be materialized on
// the selected edge" and therefore a different mechanism from the tag→INDEX arithmetic. It is not: a case
// NAME is an IMMORTAL `.rdata` String, i.e. a machine word that is constant per case exactly as an index is,
// so the identical branchless `Σᵢ constantᵢ × (tag == tagᵢ)` selects it — see
// `Parser.emitEnumCaseConstantSelect`, which is the ONE emitter all three read through.
let EnumOrdinalMemberName = b"ordinal"

// ⭐ **`s.name` — THE CASE'S SPELLING, AS A `String`.** The constant selected is `EnumLayout.caseNames[i]`,
// materialized as the same immortal `.rdata` record a source literal of those bytes materializes, so it is
// never allocated, refcounted or dropped and may be returned out of the function that read it.
let EnumNameMemberName = b"name"

// ⭐ **`s.rawValue` — WHAT THE CASE WAS DECLARED AS, IN THE DOMAIN ITS BACKING NAMES.** The one accessor whose
// RESULT TYPE depends on `EnumLayout.backing`: an `int` for an integer (or unnumbered) backing, a `float` for
// a float one, a `Character` for a char one, a `String` for a string one. For the three backings whose tag IS
// the raw value (`tagCarriesRawValue`) the tag is the answer, decoded into that domain; for a string backing
// the tag is only a declaration index and the value rides `caseRawValues`, selected the same way `.name` is.
export let EnumRawValueMemberName = b"rawValue"

// ⭐⭐ **THE THREE MEMBERS AN ENUM *VALUE* CARRIES, AS A CLASSIFICATION RATHER THAN AS A LIST OF NAMES.**
// Two parties read it — the accessor door's cursor predicate (`Parser.enumAccessorFieldAt`, which only asks
// whether the member is one of them) and the accessor itself (`Parser.parseEnumAccessor`, which has to
// answer for each) — and they must not be able to disagree about the set.
//
// ⛔ **A PREDICATE PLUS AN `if` CHAIN CANNOT DELIVER THAT, AND THE FAILURE IS A RUNTIME PANIC.** Spelled as
// an `or` over three names beside a chain of three `if`s, a fourth member added to the predicate reaches the
// accessor's unreachable arm — a compiler PANIC in front of a user, at run time, for a member the predicate
// itself invited. As a union the accessor's `match` is exhaustive, so the fourth member cannot be admitted
// without the accessor refusing to COMPILE until it is answered. It is `CheckedDivisionKind`'s shape one
// directory over, and its header states the rule this borrows: *"a fourth kind cannot be named as throwing
// without the lowering refusing to compile."*
export union EnumInstanceAccessor
	// `.ordinal` — the case's DECLARATION INDEX.
	ordinal

	// `.name` — the case's SPELLING, as a `String`.
	caseName

	// `.rawValue` — what the case was declared as, in the domain its backing names.
	rawValue

	// The member names none of the three. The door declines it and `requireStructBase`'s refusal stands,
	// which is still the true answer for every other member of an enum.
	notAnAccessor
end 'EnumInstanceAccessor'

// THE classifier — the one place a member NAME becomes one of the three, so the door's cursor test and the
// accessor's dispatch read one answer.
export function enumInstanceAccessorOf(member ByteArray) returns EnumInstanceAccessor
	if member.equals(EnumOrdinalMemberName) 'ordinal'
		return EnumInstanceAccessor.ordinal
	end 'ordinal'

	if member.equals(EnumNameMemberName) 'caseName'
		return EnumInstanceAccessor.caseName
	end 'caseName'

	if member.equals(EnumRawValueMemberName) 'rawValue'
		return EnumInstanceAccessor.rawValue
	end 'rawValue'

	return EnumInstanceAccessor.notAnAccessor
end 'enumInstanceAccessorOf'

// An exhaustive `match` and never `!= notAnAccessor`: a member appended to `EnumInstanceAccessor`
// must be classified here too, so the cursor door cannot silently start admitting one that
// `Parser.parseEnumAccessor` has not been taught to answer.
export function namesAnEnumInstanceAccessor(accessor EnumInstanceAccessor) returns bool
	match accessor 'a'
		ordinal or
			caseName or
			rawValue then return true
		notAnAccessor then return false
	end 'a'
end 'namesAnEnumInstanceAccessor'

// ⭐ **`<Enum>.allCases` — EVERY CASE, IN DECLARATION ORDER, AS AN `Array with <Enum>`.** The STATIC sibling
// of `allCaseNames` and deliberately its twin in shape: an ordinary array value of the type the language
// already has, built op-for-op like the source literal `[E.a, E.b]` — so `.count()`, `.get(0)` and
// `for c in …` reach it through the ordinary postfix and loop machinery with no accessor protocol of its own.
// See `Parser.emitEnumAllCases`.
export let EnumAllCasesMemberName = b"allCases"

// ⭐ **`<Enum>.fromRawValue(raw)` — THE REVERSE LOOKUP, AND THE ONE MEMBER OF THIS SURFACE THAT THROWS.**
// A raw value naming no case is a runtime failure, so unlike its four siblings it cannot be an expression
// the parser expands in place: the compiler's `try` binds to a CALL (`Parser.rewriteLastCallToTryCall`), so this one
// is a per-enum Std helper the parser calls. See `Runtime/EnumLookupRuntime.maxon`.
export let EnumFromRawValueMemberName = b"fromRawValue"

// ⭐ **`<Enum>.fromName(name)` / `<Union>.fromName(name, payload…)` — THE LOOKUP BY CASE NAME, AND
// `fromRawValue`'s TWIN IN SHAPE BUT NOT IN SCOPE.** A name naming no case is a runtime failure, so like its
// sibling it is a per-declaration Std helper the parser CALLS rather than an expression the parser expands
// — the compiler's `try` binds to a call (`Parser.rewriteLastCallToTryCall`). See `Runtime/EnumLookupRuntime.maxon`.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS ON EVERY DECLARATION, WHERE `fromRawValue` IS ENUM-ONLY AND REACHES ONLY TWO OF THE SEVEN
// BACKINGS — and the asymmetry is not an inconsistency, it is what the two members ASK.** `fromRawValue`
// searches the case TAG column, which carries the declared raw values only for an `integer`-backed or
// unnumbered (`unresolved`) enum; the four in between (`float`, `character`, `string`, `functionRef`) are
// refused by name (E2015, `Parser.requireFromRawValueBacking`), `structRecord` is an absence rather than a
// refusal, and a union is never claimed at all. `fromName` searches the case NAME column, which EVERY
// declaration has in full, whatever its keyword and whatever its backing — and hands back
// `EnumLayout.tagOfIndex`, which IS a case's runtime representation for all seven. So there is no backing to
// refuse and no union to exclude.
export let EnumFromNameMemberName = b"fromName"

// ⭐⭐ **`<Union>.unionCases` — THE COMPILER-SYNTHESIZED COMPANION ENUM OF A PAYLOAD-BEARING UNION**
// (`/specs/union-cases.md`). One bare case per variant of the union, in declaration order, rawValue = index:
// it exposes the union's DISCRIMINANT as a first-class enum value, so a decoder can write the variant's
// `rawValue` to a buffer and lift it back through `fromRawValue` — and adding a variant breaks the match in
// the writer AND the reader.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS SYNTHESIZED ONLY FOR A UNION WITH ASSOCIATED VALUES.** The gate is
// `appendUnionCasesCompanion`'s and so is its reason — stated there once, INCLUDING what the reason is not.
// A second copy here would be free to go wrong without anything failing.
export let UnionCasesMemberName = b"unionCases"

// ⭐ **THE COMPANION'S REGISTERED NAME — `<Union>.unionCases`, the spelling the author writes.** A DOTTED
// name, which is what makes `function describe(k Shape.unionCases)` resolve with no new type grammar: the
// qualified arm of `Parser.parseTypeReference` already assembles a dotted chain into one `named(...)`, so
// the companion is found under exactly the key a type reference to it produces. The alternative, a
// reserved `__`-prefixed mint, would need a translation at every door the author's spelling arrives
// through.
export function unionCasesCompanionName(unionName ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	return "{String.from(unionName)}.{String.from(UnionCasesMemberName)}".toByteArray()
end 'unionCasesCompanionName'

// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO SYNTHESIZED SERVICE COMPANIONS' REGISTERED NAMES — `<T>.request` and `<T>.handle`.**
// DOTTED, on `unionCasesCompanionName`'s precedent and for its exact reason: `Parser.parseTypeReference`'s
// qualified arm already assembles a dotted chain into one `named(…)`, so `function serve(h Calc.handle)`
// resolves with no new type grammar and the companion is found under exactly the key a type reference to it
// produces. A reserved `__`-prefixed mint would need a translation at every door the author's spelling
// arrives through — and the author's spelling is the one the design document teaches.
let ServiceRequestMemberName = b"request"
let ServiceHandleMemberName = b"handle"

export function serviceRequestCompanionName(typeName ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	return "{String.from(typeName)}.{String.from(ServiceRequestMemberName)}".toByteArray()
end 'serviceRequestCompanionName'

export function serviceHandleCompanionName(typeName ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	return "{String.from(typeName)}.{String.from(ServiceHandleMemberName)}".toByteArray()
end 'serviceHandleCompanionName'

// The member half of the DISPATCH LOOP's symbol — `__`-prefixed for `ServiceShutdownCaseName`'s reason: no
// source may declare it (E2051) and no author may call it.
let ServiceLoopMemberName = b"__loop"

// ⭐⭐ **`<T>.__loop` — THE SYNTHESIZED DISPATCH LOOP'S SYMBOL, SPELLED ONCE FOR ITS THREE READERS.**
// `ServiceLoop.installServiceLoops` BUILDS the function under this name,
// `LowerMaxonToStd.lowerServiceSpawn` emits the `funcAddr` that hands its address to `__svc_spawn`, and
// `DeadFunctionElimination` keeps it alive through exactly that edge. Three spellings would be three chances
// to build one symbol and root another — a link failure at best, and at worst a service whose loop is pruned.
//
// It is `qualifiedMethodName`'s shape, so it reads as what it is: a member of `<T>` the compiler wrote.
module function serviceLoopSymbol(typeName ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	return qualifiedMethodName(typeName, methodName: ServiceLoopMemberName)
end 'serviceLoopSymbol'

// The member half of the ABANDON function's symbol — `__`-prefixed for `ServiceLoopMemberName`'s reason.
let ServiceAbandonMemberName = b"__abandon"

// ⭐⭐ **`<T>.__abandon` — WHAT A REQUEST THAT WILL NEVER BE HANDLED GETS, SPELLED ONCE FOR ITS THREE
// READERS.** `ServiceLoop.buildServiceAbandon` BUILDS it, `LowerMaxonToStd.lowerServiceSpawn` emits the
// `funcAddr` that stores its address into the mailbox's `abandonFn`, and `ServiceLoop.buildServiceDrain`
// calls it by name on the way out.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS NOT A BARE `__destruct_<T>.request`, AND THE DIFFERENCE IS A LIVENESS OBLIGATION.** The
// request union's own drop cascade frees an unhandled message's payloads and stops, but a message
// that dies unprocessed also has an AWAITER, and leaving it parked for a reply that will never come
// is a hang with no diagnostic — so the abandon path answers `ServiceError.stopped` into the reply
// slot FIRST and then drops the payloads through that same cascade.
module function serviceAbandonSymbol(typeName ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	return qualifiedMethodName(typeName, methodName: ServiceAbandonMemberName)
end 'serviceAbandonSymbol'

// ⭐⭐ **`__shutdown` IS VARIANT 0 OF EVERY REQUEST UNION, SO ADDING A MESSAGE NEVER RENUMBERS IT.** The
// poison pill is the one variant the RUNTIME will construct from a literal tag, and the roster above it is
// the author's to reorder; putting it last would move it every time a method was added. It wears the `__`
// prefix because no source may declare it (`requireUnreservedName`, E2051) and no `match` an author writes
// may name it.
export let ServiceShutdownCaseName = b"__shutdown"

// The variant INDEX that name occupies, which is what a reader that has a layout in hand actually needs.
// Derived from nothing and written here beside the rule that fixes it: `buildServiceRequestUnion` adds the
// pill FIRST, and `addServiceRequestCase` gives each case the index its position in the roster gives it.
export let ServiceShutdownCaseIndex = 0 as CaseIndex

// ⭐⭐ **THE REPLY SLOT IS AN `Integer`, DELIBERATELY, AND THE PAYLOAD CASCADE IS WHY.** It is a raw
// cell pointer, NOT a managed box: the `__destruct_<U>` payload cascade must SKIP it, and it does
// so structurally because a scalar payload is not managed. Answering an abandoned reply cell is
// `<T>.__abandon`'s job, not a decref's — and a managed-typed slot here would hand the cascade a
// box it must not free.
//
// ⚠ **A VARIANT EITHER HAS THIS SLOT OR DOES NOT, AND THERE IS NO `0` STANDING FOR "NO REPLY".**
// The presence of the slot IS the question, and `ServiceCompanions.serviceReplySlotIndex` is the
// one place it is asked. A zero as a second spelling of the same fact would be a sentinel every
// reader had to remember not to dereference.
module let ServiceReplySlotName = b"__reply"

// ⭐ **THE TRANSPORT HALF OF EVERY REPLY'S ERROR TYPE, AS A NAME.** `stdlib/Builtins.maxon` declares
// `public enum ServiceError`, and the compiler has to look that declaration up to place its cases on a
// reply's fused dispatch line — so the spelling is here, once, rather than as a byte literal at each of the
// three sites that ask (`ServiceCompanions.serviceReplyTransportPlacement`,
// `Parser.serviceReplyErrorMembers` and `ProgramSignatures.serviceReplyErrorName`).
module let ServiceErrorTypeName = b"ServiceError"

// ⭐ **THE ONE CASE OF `ServiceError`, AS A NAME.** `<T>.__abandon` transcribes its ORDINAL into a reply's
// error word, which makes the case ORDER a wire format — so the transcription is checked against the
// declaration on every compile (`BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck`), exactly as the three `__Managed*Error`
// families' are, and this constant is what names the case at both ends of that check.
module let ServiceErrorStoppedCaseName = b"stopped"

// ⭐ **THE MEMBER HALF OF A TWO-MEMBER REPLY ERROR TYPE'S NAME** — `Calc.divide.errors`,
// `specs/services.md`'s own spelling.
//
// ⛔ **IT IS A REGISTERED TYPE AND NOT MERELY A DIAGNOSTIC STRING** — so *"no registry holds it, nothing
// resolves it, and that is what makes a bare `try` over such a reply E3059 — there is no `throws` clause an
// author could write that names it"* is false in every clause.
// `ServiceCompanions.synthesizeServiceCompanions`' third pass mints a nominal enum under this
// name for every THROWING message and registers it through the same `recordEnum` door every author-written
// enum takes, so `function fetch(…) throws Calc.divide.errors` resolves and
// `Promise with (Integer, Calc.divide.errors)` interns. E3059 is what a bare `try` earns when the
// enclosing clause names the HANDLER half alone — a name mismatch, which is a different sentence for a
// different mistake.
let ServiceReplyErrorSuffix = b"errors"

// ⭐ **THE FUSED REPLY ERROR TYPE'S REGISTERED NAME — `<Service>.<method>.errors`.** DOTTED for
// `serviceRequestCompanionName`'s reason and by the same construction: `Parser.parseTypeReference`'s
// qualified arm and `Parser.parseOptionalThrowsClause` both assemble a dotted chain into one name, so the
// enum is found under exactly the key a `throws Calc.divide.errors` clause produces.
//
// ⚠ **ITS ARGUMENT IS THE MESSAGE KEY (`<Service>.<method>`), not a service name** — the fused pair is a
// property of ONE message, because what it fuses is `ServiceError` with what THAT message declares.
export function serviceReplyErrorsCompanionName(messageKey ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	return "{String.from(messageKey)}.{String.from(ServiceReplyErrorSuffix)}".toByteArray()
end 'serviceReplyErrorsCompanionName'

// The one field of `<T>.handle`: the mailbox pointer. A real 8-byte box on `managedFileLayout`'s shape
// rather than a parse-time mark, so moves, E3102, struct fields, arrays and the drop cascade all reach it
// by construction rather than through a special case at each door.
module let ServiceHandleMailboxFieldName = b"mailbox"

// Its FIELD INDEX, for the one reader that loads it through a `StructLayout` rather than at a raw offset
// (`Parser.emitServiceSendOp`). `buildServiceHandleStruct` adds exactly this one field, and the audit there
// is what keeps the index and `ServiceHandleMailboxOffset` two spellings of one geometry.
export let ServiceHandleMailboxFieldIndex = 0 as FieldIndex

// ⭐⭐ **THE HANDLE BOX'S GEOMETRY AS A NUMBER, FOR THE ONE READER THAT HAS NO LAYOUT — the RUNTIME.**
// `__mbox_handle_clone_box` mints a fresh handle box and `__mbox_handle_drop_box` reads the mailbox out of
// one, and neither can ask `StructLayout` anything: they are Std-tier bodies built after the pipeline. The
// pair is AUDITED against the layout at the one place that builds it
// (`ServiceCompanions.buildServiceHandleStruct`), so a field added to the handle fails the compile rather
// than making the runtime read past the record's end.
// ⭐⭐ **`shutdown` IS THE HANDLE'S OWN METHOD, NOT A MESSAGE — AND IT YIELDS TO ONE OF THAT NAME.** It
// enqueues the `__shutdown` pill BEHIND everything already queued, which is what makes a shutdown a graceful
// drain rather than a kill. It follows `clone`'s precedent exactly (`Parser.structCloneIsSynthesized`): a
// service that declares an `export function shutdown()` of its own WINS, because that declaration is a
// VARIANT of the request union and the dispatch looks for a variant first — which leaves dropping the last
// handle as the other road to the same drain.
//
// ⚠ It is the AUTHOR-SPELLABLE name; `ServiceShutdownCaseName` above is the compiler's `__`-prefixed
// VARIANT, which no source may declare and no author may name.
export let ServiceShutdownMethodName = b"shutdown"

export let ServiceHandleMailboxOffset = 0 as ByteOffset
export let ServiceHandleBoxSize = 8 as ParsedInt

// ⭐⭐ **SYNTHESIZE `<Union>.unionCases` BESIDE THE UNION THAT WAS JUST READ** (`/specs/union-cases.md`).
// One bare case per variant, in declaration order, tag = index — which makes it an ordinary payload-free
// enum in every respect, and that is the whole design: it is APPENDED TO THE SAME `EnumLayoutArray` the
// union itself goes into, so `Shape.unionCases.circle`, `match k`, `.allCases`, `.fromRawValue`, `.name`,
// `.rawValue` and `.ordinal` all reach it through the doors an author-written enum reaches them through.
// Nothing about the companion is special-cased past this function and `Parser.unionCasesCompanionOf`'s hop.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS BORN AT THE DECLARATION AND NOT AT FIRST USE.** The whole-program index is built by a
// TOLERANT DECLARATION SWEEP that runs before any body is parsed, and a type named in a PARAMETER
// position (`function describe(k Shape.unionCases)`) has to be resolvable from that index. A
// companion minted lazily on the first `.unionCases` parsed would exist only in files that had
// already parsed a use of it.
//
// ⭐ **TWO CALLERS, TWO REGISTRIES, ONE FUNCTION — and both are required.** The sweep's array feeds
// `ProgramSignatures.enumTypes` (what the PARSER resolves `Shape.unionCases.circle` and `match k` through);
// the real parse's `FileParseArtifact.enumTypes` feeds `Project.enumTypes` (what `TypeResolution` erases the
// named type through, what `SemanticCheck`'s argument identity reads, and what the Std installer walks).
// ⛔ MEASURED with only the first: `describe(c)` was refused *"expected 'Shape.unionCases', got 'int'"* —
// the argument's own aggregate name came back empty — and `__enum_from_raw_Shape.unionCases` was never
// synthesized, taking the x64 backend down at `resolveCallFixups`.
//
// ⚠ **ONLY FOR A UNION WITH ASSOCIATED VALUES.** `isBoxed` is that question — a union is boxed exactly when
// some case declares a payload. The gate lives HERE rather than at either caller, so the two cannot come to
// disagree about which unions have one, and `UnionCasesMemberName`'s note points at this paragraph instead of
// keeping a second copy of it.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE REASON IS NOT THAT A PAYLOAD-FREE UNION ALREADY HAS THIS SURFACE — IT HAS NEITHER HALF OF IT.**
// This comment said so twice and credited the spec both times. `/specs/union-cases.md` says *"Plain ENUMS (no
// payloads) already expose `.allCases` / `.fromRawValue` directly"*; the widening from "enum" to "enum or
// union" was this comment's own, and it is false for a union of ANY shape, because both refusals key on the
// declaration keyword rather than on payloads (`Parser.parseEnumLiteral`). MEASURED on `union Level low = 1
// high = 2 end`, a payload-free scalar-backed union: `.allCases` is **E4006** *allCases is not available on
// unions* and `.fromRawValue` is **E3034** *unknown union case*.
// `specs/union-scalar-backing.md:17-19` states that rule for unions outright — the helpers *"remain an
// `enum`-only surface"*.
//
// ⇒ **What the exclusion is actually about is what a companion would be FOR.** Its purpose is to expose a
// union's DISCRIMINANT as a first-class enum value (see `UnionCasesMemberName`), and a payload-free union's
// cases ARE first-class values carrying their own tag already — so there is nothing for a second type to
// name, while a BOXED union's discriminant is reachable no other way.
// ⇒ ⚠ **A payload-free union therefore has no route to `.allCases` at all**, and that is the
// language's rule, not a consequence of this gate. Do not "restore" a direct route here (the
// refusal is `parseEnumLiteral`'s and deliberate) and do not delete the gate to grant one.
//
// ⚠ It must be called with the layout AFTER every case is read, because the companion's case list IS the
// union's.
export function appendUnionCasesCompanion(enums EnumLayoutArray, layout EnumLayout)
	if not layout.isBoxed() 'noAssociatedValues'
		return
	end 'noAssociatedValues'

	enums.push(buildUnionCasesCompanion(layout))
end 'appendUnionCasesCompanion'

// ⭐ **THE COMPANION ITSELF, SPLIT FROM THE APPENDER FOR ITS SECOND REGISTRAR.** A SERVICE's
// request union is registered through `ProgramSignatures.recordServiceRequest` rather than pushed onto an
// array, and it owes its `unionCases` companion exactly as an author's union does —
// `services.unioncases-tags-the-request-variants` reads `Calc.request.unionCases.bump.rawValue`. Two builders
// would be two answers to "what tag does a variant carry", which is the drift the header below is entirely
// about.
export function buildUnionCasesCompanion(layout EnumLayout) returns EnumLayout
	// ⛔⛔ **THE COMPANION'S TAG IS THE UNION'S TAG, READ THROUGH `tagOfIndex` — NEVER THE LOOP INDEX.** It was
	// written `tag: i, rawValue: "{i}"`, and that was true only while a boxed union could not declare raw
	// values: every tag WAS its declaration index, so the index was the union's tag spelled differently. The
	// day `Parser.readEnumCaseInto` began reading a payload list and a raw value as independent halves
	// (`/specs/union-struct-backing.md` needs both on one case), `union Instr add(dest ID, src ID) = 5 nop = 7
	// end` became expressible and the two numbers came apart.
	//
	// ⛔ **MEASURED, and it BREAKS THE COMPANION'S STATED PURPOSE** — `/specs/union-cases.md`: *"write the
	// variant's `rawValue` to a buffer …; on read, lift the raw `int` back to a `U.unionCases` via
	// `fromRawValue`"*. On that union, `op.rawValue` answered **5** while `Instr.unionCases.add.rawValue`
	// answered **0**, so `fromRawValue(op.rawValue)` THREW. Both halves are separately pinned by the corpus and
	// nothing COMPOSED them, which is why a full green suite said nothing. The rule is to mirror the
	// parent case's ordinal so `.rawValue` agrees with the union's tag value — the natural pairing for
	// de/serialization.
	//
	// ⇒ **BYTE-NEUTRAL FOR EVERY DECLARATION THAT COULD EXIST BEFORE**, whose tags were `0..n-1` — which is
	// what makes this a latent bug being closed rather than a behaviour change.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **AND THE BACKING MUST TRAVEL WITH THE TAG, BUT ONLY WHERE THE TAG *IS* THE RAW VALUE.** Copying
	// `layout.backing` unconditionally is WRONG and the classifier says why: a `string` or `functionRef`
	// backing keeps its value in `caseRawValues`, a column the companion does not carry, so a string-backed
	// companion would answer `.rawValue` with the TEXT OF ITS TAG (`"0"`) instead of `"alpha"`. `structRecord`
